## Dependency-ordered A/B pairs

A is the proposed theorem/definition supplier; B holds examples/counterexamples. All IDs remain proposals.

### SME-01 — Scientific model contracts and applicability
**Pages:** scientific-statistical-model-contracts / scientific-statistical-model-contracts-examples. **A:** Reuse MSF-01/02 definitions. Define the observation space, sample or assignment units, response and predictor variables, index/time origin, fixed/random/observed/latent/conditioned quantities, target population and estimand, observation mechanism, support, and the probability law or assignment distribution. State whether inference conditions on the design or averages over its randomization. Mark scientific adequacy assumptions as design-controlled, empirically assessable, or substantively untestable. These labels do not imply identifiability and the formal contract proves no physical applicability. **Proof/B:** bookkeeping consistency only; compare technical/biological units, repeated visits and unvalidated calibration. **Requires:** MSF-01/02; def-random-element-and-real-random-variable and def-law-or-distribution-of-a-random-element. **Sources:** Wickham pp. 2–3; Duke STA610 Lecture 1 pp. 1–4.

### SME-02 — Fixed-design regression, estimability, and Gauss–Markov
**Pages:** fixed-design-linear-regression-and-estimability / fixed-design-linear-regression-and-estimability-examples. **A:** For fixed $X\in\mathbb R^{n\times p}$ and $Y=X\beta+\epsilon$, define least-squares estimators as $\arg\min_b\|Y-Xb\|_2^2$. The fitted vector $P_{\operatorname{col}X}Y$ and residual are unique; coefficient minimizers form $\hat\beta+\ker X$. A linear contrast $c^T\beta$ is estimable iff $c\in\operatorname{row}(X)$, equivalently $c=X^Ta$ for some $a$ so that $a^TY$ is unbiased under $E\epsilon=0$. For full-column-rank $X$, $\hat\beta=(X^TX)^{-1}X^TY$. Under fixed full-rank $X$, $E\epsilon=0$, and $\operatorname{Cov}(\epsilon)=\sigma^2I_n$ with $\sigma^2>0$, prove OLS is BLUE for estimable contrasts; Gaussianity is not needed. **Proof:** use the published least-squares normal equation, then write any linear unbiased contrast estimate as $a^TY$, $X^Ta=c$, and cancel the covariance cross-term against the OLS representative. **B:** collinearity, estimable contrast, heteroskedasticity, leverage/diagnostics. **Requires:** SME-01; MSF-01/02; thm-least-squares-solutions-and-normal-equation, thm-orthogonal-projection-is-the-unique-nearest-point, thm-rank-nullity, and the published first/second-moment identities. **Sources:** MIT 18.443 regression pp. 1–4; Wickham pp. 2–6, proof pp. 5–6; diagnostics pp. 8, 11–12, 17.

### SME-03 — Exact Gaussian regression inference and prediction
**Pages:** exact-gaussian-linear-regression-inference / exact-gaussian-linear-regression-inference-examples. **A:** Fixed full-column-rank $X\in\mathbb R^{n\times p}$, $1\le p<n$, and $\epsilon\sim N_n(0,\sigma^2I_n)$, $\sigma^2>0$. QR plus MSF-35 yields independent coefficient and residual coordinates and $RSS_F/\sigma^2\sim\chi^2_{n-p}$. For $c\ne0$, $c^T\hat\beta$ has an exact $t_{n-p}$ pivot after division by $s\sqrt{c^T(X^TX)^{-1}c}$, $s^2=RSS_F/(n-p)$. For a nested reduced design $X_R$ of rank $p-q$ with $1\le q\le p$, define $RSS_R=\|Y-P_{\operatorname{col}X_R}Y\|^2$, $RSS_F=\|Y-P_{\operatorname{col}X}Y\|^2$; under its null restriction prove $\{(RSS_R-RSS_F)/q\}/\{RSS_F/(n-p)\}\sim F_{q,n-p}$. Distinguish the Gaussian MLE $RSS_F/n$ from $s^2$. A mean-response interval at $x_0$ uses variance factor $x_0^T(X^TX)^{-1}x_0$; an independent future response with the same Gaussian error law adds $1$ inside that factor. **Proof/B:** specialize the orthogonal-coordinate theorem, then use test inversion; contrast mean-response and prediction intervals and include a non-Gaussian error counterexample. **Requires:** SME-02; MSF-08/10/35; thm-qr-factorisation-over-r-or-c and the published Gaussian/product-law clauses (AC inherited from the Gaussian definition). **Sources:** MIT 18.443 regression pp. 2–7 and MVN pp. 3–4; Wickham pp. 6–7 states, not derives, independence.

### SME-04 — Generalized linear models and Fisher scoring
**Pages:** generalized-linear-models-and-fisher-scoring / generalized-linear-models-and-fisher-scoring-examples. **A:** For independent $Y_i$ under a stated product sampling law, define exponential-family density/mass $f_i(y;\theta_i,\phi)=\exp\{(y\theta_i-b(\theta_i))/a_i(\phi)+c_i(y,\phi)\}$ relative to its base measure; state common-dispersion restrictions when used. Define $\mu_i=b'(\theta_i)$, variance $a_i(\phi)b''(\theta_i)$, link $g(\mu_i)=\eta_i=x_i^T\beta$, and canonical link $\theta_i=\eta_i$. Under common scale $a(\phi)>0$, the canonical score is $X^T(y-\mu)/a(\phi)$ and Hessian is $-X^T\operatorname{diag}(b''(\eta_i))X/a(\phi)$; strictly positive $b''(\eta_i)$ and full column rank make $\ell$ strictly concave, yielding at most one finite stationary maximizer, not existence. Define Fisher scoring/IRLS at finite $\beta$: $z_i=\eta_i+(y_i-\mu_i)/(d\mu_i/d\eta_i)$, $w_i=(d\mu_i/d\eta_i)^2/\{a(\phi)V(\mu_i)\}>0$, and output a weighted least-squares minimizer; prove this equals the Fisher-scoring step when information is invertible. No termination/global-convergence guarantee. Define deviance $D=2\{\sup_{\beta\in\Theta_{\rm sat}}\ell(\beta)-\sup_{\beta\in\Theta_{\rm fit}}\ell(\beta)\}$ only for nested stated models on common support, so it is nonnegative by set inclusion; no residual distribution or goodness-of-fit reference law is claimed. Score/information identities require common support, differentiability and integrable derivative envelopes. MSF-15 applies only to a matching fixed-dimensional iid random-design model with its exact interior/regularity assumptions. For deterministic independent nonidentically distributed responses, omit calibration unless a local triangular-array score/Hessian theorem is proved with Lindeberg, normalized design-information convergence, no dominating observation, and uniform Taylor remainder conditions. No generic saturated-model chi-square or deviance-residual law. **B:** logistic separation, Poisson overdispersion, identity-link boundary. **Requires:** SME-01; MSF-04/05/07; MSF-15 only for the matching iid random-design route; published matrix differentiation. **Sources:** MIT 18.650 pp. 10–21, 23–33, 40–52; CMU Tibshirani pp. 1–5. Deviance diagnostics source gap remains.

### SME-05 — Multivariate normal, Wishart, and Hotelling $T^2$
**Pages:** multivariate-normal-wishart-hotelling / multivariate-normal-wishart-hotelling-examples. **A:** For iid $X_i\sim N_p(\mu,\Sigma)$, $\Sigma\succ0$, define $\bar X=n^{-1}\sum_iX_i$ and $S=(n-1)^{-1}\sum_i(X_i-\bar X)(X_i-\bar X)^T$. For $n>p$, prove $\bar X$ and $S$ independent and $(n-1)S\sim W_p(\Sigma,n-1)$, defining the Wishart law as the law of $\sum_{j=1}^{n-1}Z_jZ_j^T$ for iid $Z_j\sim N_p(0,\Sigma)$. Then $T^2=n(\bar X-\mu_0)^TS^{-1}(\bar X-\mu_0)$ satisfies $(n-p)T^2/\{p(n-1)\}\sim F_{p,n-p}$ under $H_0:\mu=\mu_0$. Under $H_0:\Sigma=\Sigma_0$, the distinct exact trace statistic $\operatorname{tr}(\Sigma_0^{-1}(n-1)S)\sim\chi^2_{p(n-1)}$; the covariance likelihood-ratio statistic has a separate regular fixed-dimensional asymptotic calibration with $p(p+1)/2$ degrees of freedom under MSF-15's matching iid/interior premises. **Proof:** orthogonally decompose the $n$ sample rows into the mean coordinate and $n-1$ residual coordinates. For Hotelling, whiten by $\Sigma^{1/2}$, rotate the mean vector to $e_1$, and use the Schur-complement identity $1/(e_1^TW^{-1}e_1)=g_1^T(I-P_{G_2})g_1$; its independent $\chi^2_{n-p}$ and $\chi^2_p$ factors give the stated $F$ law. Spell out full rank a.s. and covariance conventions in the item proof. **B:** $n\le p$, singular sample covariance, exact/asymptotic distinction. **Requires:** SME-01/03; MSF-04/08/10/15/35 as appropriate; published multivariate-normal and moment clauses (AC inherited). **Sources:** Springer Ch. 3 pp. 1–87 (55–62), Ch. 5 §5.5 pp. 47–66, Ch. 6 §§6.3–6.4 pp. 20–42; Mukherjee Lectures 2–3 cross-check but defer proof parts.

### SME-06 — PCA and eigengap stability
**Pages:** principal-components-and-eigenspace-stability / principal-components-and-eigenspace-stability-examples. **A:** Define covariance-PCA as the ordered eigenspaces of $\operatorname{Cov}(X)$, and correlation-PCA as those of the covariance matrix after each coordinate is divided by its positive standard deviation. State the centering/scaling convention. A repeated leading eigenvalue identifies its invariant subspace but not a distinguished unit vector. Locally prove for symmetric $A$, simple leading eigenvalue gap $g=\lambda_1(A)-\lambda_2(A)>0$, symmetric $E$ with $\epsilon=\|E\|_2<g/2$, and leading unit eigenvectors $v,\tilde v$, that $|\lambda_1(A+E)-\lambda_1(A)|\le\epsilon$ and $\sin\angle(v,\tilde v)\le\epsilon/(g-\epsilon)$; the perturbed leading eigenvalue is simple. The proof uses Courant–Fischer and projects the perturbed eigen-equation onto $v^\perp$. For iid $X_i$ with finite fourth moments, prove $\|\hat\Sigma-\Sigma\|_F=O_P(n^{-1/2})$ entrywise by Chebyshev/finite-dimensional norm comparison; an isolated simple leading eigenvalue then gives eigenspace consistency. This is a local bound, not general Davis–Kahan or finite-sample selected-component inference. **B:** tied eigenvalues, sign ambiguity, scale changes and small gaps. **Requires:** SME-01/02; cor-real-spectral-theorem-for-self-adjoint-endomorphisms, thm-courant-fischer-min-max-principle, and published covariance algebra. Current authored A-page supplier interfaces also require `robust-covariance-and-high-dimensional-stability`. **Sources:** Springer Ch. 9 pp. 1–43, especially 2–15; Mukherjee Lecture 8 pp. 2–4; perturbation proof local.

### SME-07 — Mixed effects and longitudinal models
**Pages:** mixed-effects-and-longitudinal-models / mixed-effects-and-longitudinal-models-examples. **A:** For fixed design define $Y=X\beta+Zb+\epsilon$, $Eb=E\epsilon=0$, $\operatorname{Cov}(b)=G$, $\operatorname{Cov}(\epsilon)=R$, $b\perp\epsilon$, giving $V=\operatorname{Cov}(Y)=ZGZ^T+R$; Gaussianity is needed for the joint-normal conditional formula, not this covariance identity. With known $V\succ0$ and full-column-rank $X$, define GLS $\hat\beta=(X^TV^{-1}X)^{-1}X^TV^{-1}Y$, prove it is BLUE by whitening, and state $\operatorname{Cov}(\hat\beta)=(X^TV^{-1}X)^{-1}$. For known $\beta,G,R$ under joint Gaussianity, state $E[b\mid Y]=GZ^TV^{-1}(Y-X\beta)$ and $\operatorname{Cov}(b\mid Y)=G-GZ^TV^{-1}ZG$. For unknown fixed effects, define $\hat b=GZ^TV^{-1}(Y-X\hat\beta_{\rm GLS})$ as the BLUP and give $\operatorname{Cov}(b-\hat b)=G-GZ^TV^{-1}ZG+GZ^TV^{-1}X(X^TV^{-1}X)^{-1}X^TV^{-1}ZG$; distinguish this known-$V$ result from empirical BLUP with estimated variance components. Under the Gaussian model with fixed full-column-rank $X\in\mathbb R^{n\times p}$ and $p<n$, define REML as the likelihood of error contrasts $K^TY$, where $K\in\mathbb R^{n\times(n-p)}$ is fixed independently of the covariance parameters, has full column rank, satisfies $X^TK=0$, and has columns spanning $\operatorname{col}(X)^\perp$; their law eliminates $\beta$. With $V\succ0$, the contrast covariance $K^TVK$ is positive definite. At $p=n$, the contrast space is zero-dimensional and this error-contrast branch supplies no variance information. Variance components are identifiable iff $\theta\mapsto V(\theta)$ is injective on the stated parameter space. State subject/visit indices and missing-visit/dropout mechanism; covariance equations do not imply ignorability. **B:** random-intercept ICC, aliased slope, informative dropout. **Requires:** SME-01/02; MSF-01/02/04; MSF-03 only for explicit prediction-risk comparisons; published `def-multivariate-normal-law`, `thm-normal-equations-for-best-affine-l2-prediction`, `thm-least-squares-solutions-and-normal-equation`, and `thm-orthogonal-projection-is-the-unique-nearest-point`. Prove the arbitrary-dimensional Gaussian block-conditioning formulas locally by a residual/Schur-complement argument, including the allowed singular latent covariance; `ex-conditional-density-of-a-bivariate-normal-law` is a cross-check only. Carry AC from the Gaussian and L2 suppliers. Current authored A-page supplier interfaces also require `direct-matrix-factorisations-lu-cholesky-and-qr`. **Sources:** Duke STA610 Lecture 1 pp. 1–11, Lecture 3 pp. 1–9, Lecture 4 pp. 1–6, Lecture 5 pp. 8–10 (states BLUP, does not prove general matrix result), Lecture 6 pp. 1–16, Lecture 7 pp. 1–21. No Bayesian or computational procedure.

### SME-08 — Finite populations and design probability
**Pages:** finite-populations-and-sampling-designs / finite-populations-and-sampling-designs-examples. **A:** Let $U=\{1,\ldots,N\}$ be fixed with fixed values $y_i$, target total $T=\sum_Uy_i$ or mean $T/N$, and sample $s\subseteq U$ drawn from a declared probability mass function $p_d(s)$. Define $I_i=1\{i\in s\}$, $\pi_i=P_d(I_i=1)$, $\pi_{ij}=P_d(I_i=I_j=1)$, and design expectation $E_d$ while holding all $y_i$ fixed. For $\pi_i>0$, $\hat T_{HT}=\sum_U I_iy_i/\pi_i$ is design-unbiased. If $\pi_i=0$, two finite populations differing only at that unit induce the same observed-data law but different totals, so no observed-sample estimator is unbiased for every finite population. Distinguish this probability law from a superpopulation model that randomizes the $y_i$. **B:** census, SRSWOR, certainty unit, unobserved unit. **Requires:** SME-01/02; MSF-01/02; published probability law, expectation linearity, and indicator expectation. Current authored A-page supplier interfaces also require `finite-sample-point-estimation-and-unbiased-optimality`. **Sources:** Shalabh Chs. 1–2 pp. 1–15; Borkowski 6.1–6.2/printed pp. 141–143.

### SME-09 — HT variance and SRSWOR
**Pages:** horvitz-thompson-variance-and-srswor / horvitz-thompson-variance-and-srswor-examples. **A:** Define $\Delta_{ij}=\pi_{ij}-\pi_i\pi_j$. If every $\pi_i>0$, prove $ \operatorname{Var}_d(\hat T_{HT})=\sum_{i,j\in U}\Delta_{ij}y_iy_j/(\pi_i\pi_j)$. The unbiased pair-inclusion estimate $\sum_{i,j\in s}(\Delta_{ij}/\pi_{ij})(y_i/\pi_i)(y_j/\pi_j)$ requires every $\pi_{ij}>0$; zero pair support does not license division or imply variance estimability. For fixed sample size, $\sum_j\Delta_{ij}=0$ yields the Sen–Yates–Grundy identity $-\tfrac12\sum_{i,j}\Delta_{ij}(y_i/\pi_i-y_j/\pi_j)^2$, this identity equals the true design variance and is always nonnegative. With positive pair inclusion, its sample inverse-pair form is unbiased. The condition $\Delta_{ij}\le0$ for all distinct pairs is sufficient for termwise nonnegative sample estimates, not necessary for a particular fixed population or, in general when $n>2$, for nonnegativity over all population values. For SRSWOR of $n$ from $N>1$, derive $\operatorname{Cov}_d(I_i,I_j)=-n(N-n)/(N^2(N-1))$ for $i\ne j$ and $\operatorname{Var}_d(\bar y)=(1-n/N)S_U^2/n$, where $S_U^2=\sum_U(y_i-\bar y_U)^2/(N-1)$. This design variance does not imply exact normal inference. **B:** zero pair inclusion, census, $n=1$. **Requires:** SME-08; published covariance bilinearity and finite-sum variance. **Sources:** Shalabh Ch. 7 pp. 23–31, Ch. 2 pp. 1–23; Borkowski 6.2 pp. 143–146; Oehlert Ch. 7. Correct Shalabh p.14 sign; exclude Borkowski p.145 PPS arithmetic. Tiwari–Nigam–Pant (2007), Survey Methodology 33(1), pp. 87–94, especially p. 90, confirms that the nonpositive-pair condition is sufficient but not necessary when $n>2$.

### SME-10 — Stratification and allocation
**Pages:** stratified-sampling-and-allocation / stratified-sampling-and-allocation-examples. **A:** Define a partition $U=\bigsqcup_hU_h$, $N_h=|U_h|$, independent within-stratum SRSWOR of $n_h$ units, and $S_h^2=\sum_{i\in U_h}(y_i-\bar y_h)^2/(N_h-1)$ for noncertainty strata with $N_h>1$. A certainty stratum $n_h=N_h$ contributes zero variance without requiring $S_h^2$. Prove $\hat T_{st}=\sum_hN_h\bar y_{h,s}$ is design-unbiased and $\operatorname{Var}_d(\hat T_{st})=\sum_{h:n_h<N_h}N_h^2(1-n_h/N_h)S_h^2/n_h$. For continuous allocations with fixed $\sum_hn_h=n$, derive $n_h\propto N_hS_h$; with per-unit cost $c_h>0$ and fixed cost budget derive $n_h\propto N_hS_h/\sqrt{c_h}$. These optima assume positive variances in noncertainty strata and no integer, lower-bound, capacity or minimum-sample constraints; handle zero-variance strata and constrained rounding separately. **B:** proportional/disproportionate allocation, zero-variance stratum, rounding. **Requires:** SME-08/09; finite covariance and constrained optimization proof local. **Sources:** Shalabh Ch. 4 complete chapter; JPSM outline is not proof.

### SME-11 — Cluster and two-stage sampling
**Pages:** cluster-and-two-stage-sampling / cluster-and-two-stage-sampling-examples. **A:** Define a primary sampling unit (PSU), secondary sampling unit (SSU), cluster total and the exact first/second-stage sample laws. For SRSWOR of $m$ clusters from $M$, prove the total estimator $M$ times the sample mean of cluster totals has variance $M^2(1-m/M)S_T^2/m$. In two-stage sampling define the expansion estimator and derive its variance by conditioning on the first-stage sample: variance of true PSU totals plus expected within-PSU sampling variance, with the stage-specific inclusion probabilities stated. Separately, for equal cluster size $m_c$, equal marginal variance, common within-cluster correlation $\rho$, and negligible FPC, derive $DEFF=\operatorname{Var}(\bar Y_{\rm cluster})/\operatorname{Var}(\bar Y_{\rm element\ SRS})=1+(m_c-1)\rho$. $\rho=0$ means no ICC inflation, not zero sampling error. Do not transfer this formula to unequal sizes/weights or nonexchangeable covariance. **B:** village/field subsampling and unequal cluster shortcut failure. **Requires:** SME-08; published covariance algebra and conditional-variance decomposition. **Sources:** Shalabh Chs. 9–10 complete; Holmes–Huber Ch.13. Correct Shalabh Ch.9 p.7 wording.

### SME-12 — Assignment randomization and sharp-null tests
**Pages:** randomized-treatment-assignment-and-sharp-null-tests / randomized-treatment-assignment-and-sharp-null-tests-examples. **A:** Define finite experimental units $i=1,\ldots,N$, assignment space $\mathcal Z$ with known probabilities $p(z)$, fixed potential-outcome schedule $Y_i(z)$, observed outcome $Y_i(Z)$, and statistic $T(z,Y(z))$. Under the Fisher sharp null $Y_i(z)=Y_i(z')$ for every $i,z,z'$, all assignment outcomes are imputable. For a predeclared upper-tail ordering, $p_{\rm rand}=\sum_{z\in\mathcal Z}p(z)1\{T(z)\ge T(Z_{\rm obs})\}$ is superuniform under the actual assignment law; $p^+=P_d(T>T_{\rm obs})+U P_d(T=T_{\rm obs})$, with independent $U\sim U(0,1)$, is uniform. State the statistic and two-sided ordering explicitly. The sharp null is not the weak null of zero average effect; random assignment supplies no Gaussian error law. **Proof:** enumerate the finite weighted orbit and use the discrete probability integral transform; for randomized ties, partition the unit interval by the statistic's atom masses. **B:** sharp versus average null, unequal assignment probabilities and ties. **Requires:** SME-01; MSF-02/08; finite-sum probability. This is a treatment-assignment theorem, not the generic finite-group orbit interface of MSF-11. **Sources:** Oehlert Ch.13; Holmes–Huber Ch.13; local design proof.

### SME-13 — Blocking, pairs, and Latin squares
**Pages:** blocking-and-paired-designs / blocking-and-paired-designs-examples. **A:** Define a complete block as containing each treatment once and specify the within-block assignment law. For pair $j$ with fixed potential outcomes $Y_{j\ell}(z)$, $\ell=1,2$, explicitly assume no interference and assign exactly one unit to $z=1$ and the other to $z=0$, uniformly between the two assignments. Put $\delta_{j\ell}=Y_{j\ell}(1)-Y_{j\ell}(0)$, $D_{j1}=Y_{j1}(1)-Y_{j2}(0)$, and $D_{j2}=Y_{j2}(1)-Y_{j1}(0)$. The observed treated-minus-control contrast has expectation $\tfrac12(\delta_{j1}+\delta_{j2})$ and design variance $(D_{j1}-D_{j2})^2/4$, including baseline-outcome differences. Separately prove that the latent effect $S_j$ of the unit randomly selected for treatment has the same expectation and variance $(\delta_{j1}-\delta_{j2})^2/4$; $S_j$ uses both potential outcomes of that unit and is not a statistic of the observed paired data. Include a counterexample to using the latent-effect variance for the observed contrast. Under $Y_{ij}=\mu+\tau_i+\beta_j+\epsilon_{ij}$ and independent mean-zero Gaussian equal-variance errors, derive the RCBD treatment contrast/F law separately; with one observation per block-treatment cell, interaction cannot be separated from residual error. Define a Latin square as a treatment-by-row-by-column allocation with each treatment once in each row and column, and state its restricted randomization law separately from an RCBD. **B:** missing treatment, treatment-by-block interaction, Latin-square allocation. **Requires:** SME-12; MSF-02; covariance algebra and MSF-35 only for exact Gaussian F inference. **Sources:** Oehlert Ch.13; NIST §5.3.3.2 pp.1303–1306; Holmes–Huber 13.4.4. NIST Latin-square pages not fully read; no extra theorem.

### SME-14 — Full factorials
**Pages:** full-factorial-contrasts-and-interactions / full-factorial-contrasts-and-interactions-examples. **A:** For one observation at each of all $2^k$ runs $x\in\{-1,+1\}^k$, define effect column $x_A=\prod_{j\in A}x_j$ for subset $A\subseteq\{1,\ldots,k\}$, with $x_\varnothing=1$. Prove $2^{-k}X^TX=I$ by sign cancellation over $A\triangle B$. In $Y=X\beta+\epsilon$, define $\hat\beta_A=2^{-k}\sum_xx_AY_x$; the usual factorial effect for nonempty $A$ is $2\hat\beta_A$. Under independent errors with common variance $\sigma^2$, derive $\operatorname{Var}(\hat\beta_A)=\sigma^2/2^k$ and the corresponding effect variance. Replication changes this by its declared run count. Orthogonality is a design-matrix fact, not additivity, zero interaction, or a physical mechanism. **B:** interaction, scale dependence, unbalanced loss. **Requires:** SME-12; finite-dimensional inner-product and covariance algebra. **Sources:** Oehlert Ch.8; NIST §5.3.3.3 pp.1320–1328.

### SME-15 — Fractional factorial aliases and foldover
**Pages:** fractional-factorial-aliasing-and-foldover / fractional-factorial-aliasing-and-foldover-examples. **A:** Define a regular $2^{k-p}$ fraction by $p$ independent generators and its defining subgroup $H$; effects $A$ and $B$ are aliased exactly when their sign columns agree on the fraction, equivalently $AB\in H$. Define the defining relation and resolution as the shortest nonidentity word in $H$. Equal alias columns cannot have separately estimated coefficients absent further runs or a sparsity/hierarchy assumption. For a complete mirror foldover that reverses all factor signs, prove a word of order $r$ changes sign by $(-1)^r$; opposite-parity aliases separate, same-parity members may remain aliased. A partial foldover separates only the targeted columns and must list residual aliases. **B:** $I=ABC$ aliases and full/partial foldover. **Requires:** SME-14; sign-product linear algebra. **Sources:** Oehlert Chs.15,18; NIST §5.3.3.4.1–.4 pp.1333–1347 and 5.3.3.8 pp.1382–1389.

### SME-16 — Response surfaces and optimal design
**Pages:** response-surfaces-and-optimal-design / response-surfaces-and-optimal-design-examples. **A:** Define a response surface as a local polynomial approximation on a declared factor region; for a second-order model $m(x)=\beta_0+x^T\beta+x^TBx$ with symmetric nonsingular $B$, derive stationary point $x_0=-\tfrac12B^{-1}\beta$ and classify it by the eigenvalue signs of $B$, only within the fitted model/region. Define a central composite design by factorial/fractional-factorial, axial and center runs. For $y=f(x)^T\beta+\epsilon$ with independent homoscedastic variance $\sigma^2$, an approximate design is weights $\xi_x\ge0,\sum_x\xi_x=1$ on a finite candidate set, $M(\xi)=\sum_x\xi_xf(x)f(x)^T$. Define D-optimality as maximizing $\log\det M$, A-optimality as minimizing $\operatorname{tr}(M^{-1})$, E-optimality as maximizing $\lambda_{\min}(M)$, and c-optimality as minimizing $c^TM^{-1}c$, on nonsingular designs (with estimability specified for singular cases). State these are different model/candidate-dependent goals. Prove the D-equivalence theorem: a nonsingular $\xi^*$ is D-optimal iff $f(x)^TM(\xi^*)^{-1}f(x)\le p$ for every candidate and equality holds on its support; necessity uses the logdet directional derivative and sufficiency concavity, with support equality by averaging to $p$. Exact integer-run optimality is a separate finite design problem. **B:** saddle, approximate versus exact design. **Requires:** SME-14/15; published determinant differential and local proof of logdet concavity. **Sources:** Oehlert Ch. 19 §§19.1–19.7; Boyd–Vandenberghe §§3.1.5 and 7.5 for logdet concavity/equivalence proof; NIST §§5.3.3.6–.9 pp. 1356–1392 supports examples only.

### SME-17 — Power and sample size
**Pages:** power-and-sample-size-planning / power-and-sample-size-planning-examples. **A:** Define test size $\sup_{\theta\in\Theta_0}P_\theta(\text{reject})$, power $P_{\theta_1}(\text{reject})$, type-II error $1-$power, target effect/precision, allocation, sidedness and feasible integer sample sizes. The planning procedure takes those inputs plus a specified sampling law/test, computes its power at each candidate positive integer size $n\in\mathbb N_{\ge1}$ and outputs the least feasible $n$ attaining the target (or reports none); it is not a universal design guarantee. For $X_i\stackrel{iid}{\sim}N(\mu,\sigma^2)$ with known $\sigma>0$ and $\alpha,\beta\in(0,1)$, testing $H_0:\mu\le\mu_0$ against $\mu_1=\mu_0+\delta$, $\delta>0$, by $Z>\Phi^{-1}(1-\alpha)$, derive power $\Phi(\delta\sqrt n/\sigma-z_{1-\alpha})$ and least integer $n\ge1$ as $\max\{1,\lceil(\sigma/\delta)^2\max\{0,z_{1-\alpha}+z_{1-\beta}\}^2\rceil\}$. If the z-quantile sum is nonpositive, every positive $n$ meets the stated target. Two-sided power includes both tails and must be computed from its stated rejection region. Unknown-$\sigma$ $t$ designs and other sampling laws require separate exact/noncentral derivations. **B:** one/two-sided and variance/effect sensitivity. **Requires:** SME-08/12; MSF-08 power definition; normal-law and quantile conventions. Current authored A-page supplier interfaces also require `characteristic-functions-inversion-and-continuity`, `reeb-stability-and-global-foliation-constructions`, `central-limit-theorems`. **Sources:** Oehlert Ch.7; NIST §7.2.2.2 pp.2019–2021.

### SME-18 — Biological and technical replication
**Pages:** biological-and-technical-replication / biological-and-technical-replication-examples. **A:** Define biological units as the independent units sampled/randomized at the intended level of generalization and technical replicates as repeated measurements of the same material/unit. Conditional on fixed treatment effects $\tau_i$ (or on a common assigned treatment level), under $Y_{ij}=\mu+\tau_i+b_i+\epsilon_{ij}$ with independent biological effects $b_i$ of variance $\sigma_b^2$ and independent technical errors $\epsilon_{ij}$ of variance $\sigma_e^2$, derive variance of the mean over $n$ independent biological units and $m$ measurements each: $\sigma_b^2/n+\sigma_e^2/(nm)$. If treatment contributions are random across units, their variance must be added under the stated assignment law. If treatment is assigned to a cage, litter, plot or batch, that assignment cluster is the randomized replicate. Technical repeats reduce the measurement component, not the number of independent biological units. **B:** five assays on one animal versus one assay on five animals. **Requires:** SME-01/07; published covariance algebra. **Sources:** Oehlert Ch.1 p.6; Holmes–Huber 13.3.2,13.4.2,13.4.5.

### SME-19 — Potential outcomes and identification
**Pages:** potential-outcomes-and-causal-identification / potential-outcomes-and-causal-identification-examples. **A:** Define treatment version $a$, potential outcome $Y^a$, observed outcome $Y=Y^A$ under consistency, target-population ATE $E(Y^1-Y^0)$ and CATE $E(Y^1-Y^0\mid L)$, conditional exchangeability $(Y^a:a\in\mathcal A)\perp A\mid L$, and positivity. The displayed point-treatment theorem is for discrete $A$ with $P(A=a)>0$ and $P(A=a\mid L)>0$ a.s. under the target $L$-law; require integrable potential outcomes. For discrete $L$, prove $E[Y^a]=\sum_l E[Y\mid A=a,L=l]P(L=l)$; state the standard-Borel RCD/integral version over $L$. For continuous $A$, RCDs identify conditional laws only for $P_A$-almost every treatment value; do not claim pointwise identification at a null value $a$ without a specified version/continuity/smoothness premise and a separate proof. Identification is constancy of the target over full-data laws compatible with the observed law and assumptions; it is not empirical plausibility. Exchangeability is generally untestable and positivity failure leaves target strata unsupported. **B:** randomized, binary confounding, positivity failure. **Requires:** MSF-01; existing def-conditional-independence-given-a-sigma-algebra, conditional expectation, and RCD/disintegration items (AC inherited by conditional expectation/RCD existence). **Sources:** H&R Ch.1 1.1–1.5 and Ch.3 3.1–3.6 printed pp.3–12,27–45; Kang strong-ignorability review.

### SME-20 — SCM, DAGs, adjustment
**Pages:** structural-causal-models-dags-and-adjustment / structural-causal-models-dags-and-adjustment-examples. **A:** Define a recursive SCM on a finite DAG $G=(V,E)$ with observed and latent endogenous nodes: $X_j=f_j(X_{\operatorname{pa}(j)},U_j)$, measurable structural functions, and an explicit exogenous law. In the Markovian expanded model require mutually independent $U_j$; state the full-joint law as a topological product of probability kernels, then obtain the observed law by marginalizing latent nodes. An observed-only DAG factorization is claimed only under causal sufficiency or the stated latent structure. Define $do(X_k=a)$ by replacing only its structural equation. On the full DAG, call an internal path node a collider when both adjacent arrows point into it, and a noncollider otherwise; let $\operatorname{De}^+(v)$ contain $v$ and all its directed descendants. A path is active given $C$ iff every noncollider lies outside $C$ and every collider has a member of $\operatorname{De}^+(v)$ in $C$; d-separation means no active path joins the queried sets. State the global Markov implication (full DAG factorization implies d-separation conditional independence); define faithfulness separately as its converse for the specified law. No Markov-equivalence or skeleton/unshielded-collider characterization is claimed here. For displayed pointwise adjustment formulas, restrict $A$ to discrete/atomic values of positive probability. For backdoor adjustment, evaluate paths in the full DAG after deleting arrows out of $A$; require $L$ contain no descendants of $A$, d-separate $A,Y$, and $P(A=a\mid L)>0$ a.s. on target support; then $P(Y\mid do(A=a))=\int P(Y\mid A=a,L=l)P_L(dl)$. For front-door, use the full graph including latent nodes and require (i) $M$ intercepts all directed $A\to Y$ paths, (ii) no open backdoor path $A$-$M$, and (iii) all backdoor paths $M$-$Y$ are blocked by $A$. Require $P(A=a)>0$ and the support conditions for nested conditional laws; in the discrete case, for each $m$ with $P(M=m\mid A=a)>0$, require $P(A=a'\mid M=m)>0$ for every $a'$ with $P(A=a')>0$. Prove the front-door functional $\int P(dm\mid A=a)\int E(Y\mid M=m,A=a')P_A(da')$. For continuous $A$, regular conditional kernels identify these formulas only for $P_A$-almost every $a$; pointwise intervention effects at a null value require a specified version plus continuity/smoothness assumptions and a separate proof. These are sufficient criteria only. **Proof:** derive the full-joint kernel factorization from independent exogenous variables in topological order; marginalize latent nodes; prove the global Markov implication by local factorization/elimination; derive adjustment formulas by intervention factorization. **B:** confounder/mediator/collider and two SCMs with the same observed law but different intervention laws. **Interfaces:** def-recursive-structural-causal-model-and-dag; def-d-separation-in-a-dag; thm-dag-factorization-implies-d-separation-conditional-independence; def-faithfulness-of-a-dag-model; thm-backdoor-adjustment-sufficiency; thm-frontdoor-adjustment-sufficiency. **Requires:** SME-19; MSF-01; existing def-conditional-independence-given-a-sigma-algebra (published, AC); probability-kernel and conditional-law items. **Sources:** Pearl 3.1–3.3 PDF pp.24–36/journal pp.119–131; H&R Chs.6–7 pp.75–107. The local proofs must not rely on faithfulness to infer missing arrows or on an unreviewed Markov-equivalence theorem.

### SME-21 — Standardization and IPW identification
**Pages:** standardization-and-ipw-identification / standardization-and-ipw-identification-examples. **A:** Under SME-19's discrete/atomic treatment scope, for $e_a(L)=P(A=a\mid L)>0$ a.s. and integrable $Y^a$, define $\int E(Y\mid A=a,L=l)\,P_L(dl)$ and prove it equals $E[Y^a]$. Also prove $E[1\{A=a\}Y/e_a(L)]=E[Y^a]$ by conditioning on $L$, consistency and exchangeability. These are population identities for known true probabilities; they imply no finite-sample stability, estimated-weight consistency, or validity after weight truncation. For continuous $A$, replace the indicator/probability by a density or another declared intervention functional; an RCD formula is only a.e. in $a$ unless extra version/regularity conditions and proof are supplied. **B:** correct adjustment, extreme weights, truncation changing target. **Requires:** SME-19; MSF-01; existing conditional-expectation tower and indicator identities (AC for the conditional-expectation route). **Sources:** H&R Ch.2 Technical Points 2.2–2.3 and Ch.3 pp.27–45.

### SME-22 — IV and LATE
**Pages:** instrumental-variables-and-late / instrumental-variables-and-late-examples. **A:** Define binary instrument $Z$, binary treatment receipt $A(z)$, potential outcome $Y(a,z)$, exclusion $Y(a,z)=Y(a)$, consistency, random-instrument independence $Z\perp(Y(0),Y(1),A(0),A(1))$, relevance $E[A\mid Z=1]\ne E[A\mid Z=0]$, and monotonicity $A(1)\ge A(0)$ a.s. With finite means, define the Wald ratio $\{E(Y\mid Z=1)-E(Y\mid Z=0)\}/\{E(A\mid Z=1)-E(A\mid Z=0)\}$. Under exclusion, independence, consistency, relevance and monotonicity, partition units into always-takers, never-takers and compliers and prove the ratio is $E[Y(1)-Y(0)\mid A(1)>A(0)]$, the LATE. Under an additional constant additive treatment effect it identifies that common effect; it is not generally the population ATE. **B:** weak instrument, exclusion failure, defiers, heterogeneous effects. **Requires:** SME-19; MSF-01; local principal-strata algebra. **Sources:** H&R Ch.16 16.1–16.6 pp.209–225; Kang encouragement/LATE proof lines 120–225,275–371; Pearl 3.5 pp.37–41.

### SME-23 — Mediation and cross-world assumptions
**Pages:** mediation-effects-and-identification / mediation-effects-and-identification-examples. **A:** Define $M(a)$ and $Y(a,m)$, controlled direct effect $E[Y(a,m)-Y(a',m)]$, natural direct effect $E[Y(a,M(a'))-Y(a',M(a'))]$, and natural indirect effect $E[Y(a,M(a))-Y(a,M(a'))]$, naming both reference levels. The standard natural-effect formula requires consistency, treatment and mediator positivity, no unmeasured treatment–outcome and mediator–outcome confounding, no treatment-induced mediator–outcome confounder, and an explicit cross-world restriction such as NPSEM-IE; FFRCISTG alone does not imply that restriction. State the exact conditional-law formula and identify its target before claiming identification. Separable/interventionist effects require meaningful component interventions and dismissible-component restrictions and define different targets. No regression coefficient alone identifies these effects. **B:** mediator confounding, treatment-induced confounding, and compatible observed laws with different cross-world effects. **Requires:** SME-19/20; MSF-01. **Sources:** H&R Ch.23 23.1–23.4/Technical Points pp.323–331; Pearl 5.1 pp.39–41.

### SME-24 — Interference and exposure mappings
**Pages:** interference-exposure-mappings-and-design-based-effects / interference-exposure-mappings-and-design-based-effects-examples. **A:** Define fixed finite-population outcomes $Y_{ij}(\mathbf z_i)$, assignment vector $\mathbf Z_i$, and partial interference as invariance to assignments outside group $i$. An exposure mapping $D_{ij}=f_{ij}(\mathbf Z_i)$ is sufficient only if $Y_{ij}(\mathbf z_i)=Y_{ij}(d)$ whenever $f_{ij}(\mathbf z_i)=d$. With $\pi_{ij}(d)=P_d(D_{ij}(\mathbf Z_i)=d)>0$, prove the design-unbiased exposure mean $N^{-1}\sum_{ij}Y_{ij}(d)$ is estimated by $N^{-1}\sum_{ij}1\{D_{ij}=d\}Y_{ij}/\pi_{ij}(d)$. For the two-stage result, define $N>1$ groups with fixed sizes $n_i$, fixed outcomes, and strategies $\psi,\phi$. First choose exactly $C\in\{1,\ldots,N-1\}$ groups uniformly for $\psi$; second, within a group assigned strategy $s$, choose a fixed $K_i^s\in\{1,\ldots,n_i-1\}$ units uniformly for treatment. Define $\bar Y_i(z;\psi)=n_i^{-1}\sum_jE_\psi[Y_{ij}(\mathbf Z_i)\mid Z_{ij}=z]$, $\bar Y(z;\psi)=N^{-1}\sum_i\bar Y_i(z;\psi)$, and $\bar Y(\psi)=N^{-1}\sum_i n_i^{-1}\sum_jE_\psi[Y_{ij}(\mathbf Z_i)]$. Define $DE(\psi)=\bar Y(0;\psi)-\bar Y(1;\psi)$, $IE(\phi,\psi)=\bar Y(0;\phi)-\bar Y(0;\psi)$, $TE(\phi,\psi)=\bar Y(0;\phi)-\bar Y(1;\psi)$, and $OE(\phi,\psi)=\bar Y(\phi)-\bar Y(\psi)$. Give the source estimators explicitly: $\hat{\bar Y}_i(z;\psi)=\sum_jY_{ij}(\mathbf Z_i)1\{Z_{ij}=z\}/\sum_j1\{Z_{ij}=z\}$, $\hat{\bar Y}(z;\psi)=\sum_i\hat{\bar Y}_i(z;\psi)1\{S_i=\psi\}/\sum_i1\{S_i=\psi\}$, $\hat{\bar Y}_i(\psi)=n_i^{-1}\sum_jY_{ij}(\mathbf Z_i)$, and $\hat{\bar Y}(\psi)=\sum_i\hat{\bar Y}_i(\psi)1\{S_i=\psi\}/\sum_i1\{S_i=\psi\}$; strategy contrasts estimate the four displayed effects. Prove unbiasedness by conditioning first on group strategy and then the uniform within-group allocation. Source variance estimators require stratified interference (outcomes are invariant to which other units receive treatment conditional on their count); their expectation equals the target variance plus a nonnegative between-unit treatment-effect heterogeneity term, so equality requires additive effects. **B:** wrong exposure map, cross-group contamination and nonadditive spillover violating the variance premise. **Requires:** SME-12; MSF-01/02; explicit finite assignment law. **Sources:** Hudgens–Halloran §§2–4 Thms.1–3/Appendix A.1–A.9; Aronow–Samii §§3–4 Lemma 4.1/Prop.4.2 and App. A.2/A.4, PDF pp.4–11 and 1927–2021 lines.

### SME-25 — Missingness and nonidentification
**Pages:** missingness-mechanisms-and-identification / missingness-mechanisms-and-identification-examples. **A:** Define full data $W$, observation-pattern variable $R$, and observed components $W_{\mathrm{obs}}(r)$. MCAR means $P(R=r\mid W=w)$ is constant in $w$; MAR means for each pattern $r$, $P(R=r\mid W=w)$ depends on $w$ only through $W_{\mathrm{obs}}(r)$; MNAR is the complement relative to this declared partition. For a dominated full-data model $p_\theta(w)$ and response model $g_\psi(r\mid w)$, state product parameter space and factorization; under MAR and parameter distinctness, prove the response factor may be omitted from likelihood inference for $\theta$, but not for every target. For binary $Y$ and response indicator $R$, set $a=P(R=1)$, $b=P(R=1,Y=1)$; under unrestricted MNAR the compatible full mean is exactly $[b,b+1-a]$, while the observed response rate and success mass remain fixed. Death may make a target undefined rather than merely unobserved. **B:** MCAR/MAR/MNAR mechanisms, two full-data laws with the same observed law, and competing death. **Requires:** MSF-01/04; existing RCD/disintegration and dominated-likelihood clauses (AC for RCD). **Sources:** H&R Ch. 8 §§8.1–8.6 and Technical Points 8.1–8.2, printed pp. 109–123, §12.6 pp. 172–173; NRC Ch. 4 pp. 47–58, 70–77 and complete Ch. 5 pp. 83–106; Mao Ch. 1 §§1.1–1.7.

### SME-26 — Missingness weights and sensitivity
**Pages:** missing-data-weighting-and-sensitivity / missing-data-weighting-and-sensitivity-examples. **A:** Define $R\in\{0,1\}$, fully observed $X$, response probability $\pi(X)=P(R=1\mid X)$, and MAR $Y\perp R\mid X$. If $\pi(X)>0$ a.s. on the target support and $E|Y|<\infty$, prove $E[RY/\pi(X)]=E[Y]$ by conditioning on $X$. This is a population identity for known $\pi$, not a finite-sample or estimated-weight stability result. Define an MNAR sensitivity parameter by a stated selection or pattern-mixture law; show the target varies across full-data laws with the same observed-data law, so the parameter is not identified without added information. A sensitivity range is not a confidence interval. Any joint treatment/observation weighting identity requires its own joint/sequential law, positivity and proof. **B:** positivity failure/sensitivity curve. **Requires:** SME-25; MSF-01/02; conditional-expectation tower (AC). **Sources:** H&R Ch. 8 §§8.1–8.6 and Technical Points 8.1–8.2, printed pp. 109–123, §12.6 pp. 172–173; NRC Chs. 4–5 as above; Mao Ch. 1 §1.12, lines 228–259.

### SME-27 — Measurement error and misclassification
**Pages:** measurement-error-and-misclassification / measurement-error-and-misclassification-examples. **A:** Define latent $X$, measured $W$, classical error $W=X+U$, Berkson error $X=W+U$, and categorical misclassification through $P(W=w\mid X=x)$. Call an error nondifferential only relative to named variables, e.g. $W\perp Y\mid X,Z$; this label alone implies no direction of bias. In $Y=\alpha+\beta X+\epsilon$, with centered $X,U,\epsilon$, $U\perp(X,\epsilon)$, $\epsilon\perp X$, finite second moments and $\operatorname{Var}(X)+\operatorname{Var}(U)>0$, derive the naive slope $\operatorname{Cov}(Y,W)/\operatorname{Var}(W)=\beta\operatorname{Var}(X)/\{\operatorname{Var}(X)+\operatorname{Var}(U)\}$. State distinct assumptions before any Berkson slope claim. Validation, replicate or known-error-law correction requires its own design/model; the observed $W$-law alone generally does not identify the latent/error variance split. **B:** classical/Berkson, differential misclassification and unidentified latent variance split. **Requires:** MSF-01; MSF-04 only for a stated error likelihood; published covariance identities. **Sources:** H&R Ch. 9 §§9.1–9.6, printed pp. 125–135; Keogh et al. STRATOS §§1–4.3.5, full text lines 104–373.

### SME-28 — Event observation, censoring, truncation
**Pages:** event-time-censoring-and-truncation / event-time-censoring-and-truncation-examples. **A:** Define event time $T$, censoring time $C$, right-censored observation $U=\min(T,C)$, and $\delta=1\{T\le C\}$; define left censoring, interval censoring $\{L<T\le R\}$, current status at inspection $C$, and left/right truncation as inclusion conditional on $T>L$ or $T<R$, respectively. For a continuous event-time law with density $f(t\mid z)$, survival $S(t\mid z)=P(T>t\mid z)$, and $T\perp C\mid Z$, derive event contribution $f(U\mid Z)^\delta S(U\mid Z)^{1-\delta}$ up to censoring-only factors. Interval observation contributes $F(R\mid Z)-F(L\mid Z)$ only for fixed or noninformative inspection endpoints; current-status $\delta=1\{T\le C\}$ contributes $F(C\mid Z)^\delta S(C\mid Z)^{1-\delta}$ under the same observation-scheme premise. For delayed entry $L$, require entry independent of $T$ conditional on stated covariates and condition the event likelihood on $T>L$, giving denominator $S(L\mid Z)$. Define left/right-truncation selection explicitly; do not reuse the censoring likelihood for a truncated sample. Informative censoring/inspection/entry needs a different model. **B:** censoring/truncation, delayed entry, informative dropout. **Requires:** SME-01; MSF-01/04; published Lebesgue–Stieltjes correspondence (Countable Choice) for distribution/atom clauses. **Sources:** Yu pp.1–11; Stanford Unit 2 pp.1–4; Slud Maryland handout pp.1–2.

### SME-29 — Parametric survival and reliability
**Pages:** parametric-survival-and-reliability / parametric-survival-and-reliability-examples. **A:** Define $S(t)=P(T>t)$, density hazard $\lambda(t)=f(t)/S(t)$ where $S(t)>0$, and cumulative hazard $\Lambda(t)=\int_0^t\lambda(u)\,du$. If $T$ is absolutely continuous and $S(0)=1$, prove $S(t)=e^{-\Lambda(t)}$; state the separate product/jump relation for atoms. Define $T\sim\mathrm{Exp}(\lambda)$ by $S(t)=e^{-\lambda t}$, and Weibull(shape $k>0$, scale $\eta>0$) by $S(t)=e^{-(t/\eta)^k}$. For iid $T_i\sim\mathrm{Exp}(\lambda)$, $U_i=\min(T_i,C_i)$, $\delta_i=1\{T_i\le C_i\}$, and censoring independent of event times with a parameter-distinct censor law, derive likelihood in $\lambda$ proportional to $\lambda^d e^{-\lambda\sum_iU_i}$, $d=\sum_i\delta_i$. If $d>0$ and total exposure is positive, $\hat\lambda=d/\sum_iU_i$; if $d=0$, the likelihood supremum is at $\lambda\downarrow0$, attained only if the parameter space includes the boundary $0$. Repairable-system recurrent failure counts are not a single-lifetime survival model. **B:** constant/Weibull hazard and censored exposure. **Requires:** SME-28; MSF-01/04; published CDF/measure correspondence (Countable Choice). **Sources:** NIST reliability Ch.8 pp.4–19,155–163; Stanford Unit 2 pp.1–7; Yu pp.1–11.

### SME-30 — Counting-process compensators, integrals, brackets
**Pages:** counting-process-compensators-and-integrals / counting-process-compensators-and-integrals-examples. **A, new earlier Cat-2 supplier:** On a finite horizon $[0,T]$, define a simple unit-jump counting process $N$, filtration, predictable $Y$, nonnegative predictable intensity $\lambda$, and continuous increasing $A_t=\int_0^tY_s\lambda_s\,ds$ with $A_0=0$ and $E[A_T]<\infty$. The model contract is that $M=N-A$ is a square-integrable martingale; do not claim a general compensator-existence theorem from a displayed intensity. Prove $\langle M\rangle=A$, $[M]=N$, and for bounded predictable step $H$, $\int_0^tH_s\,dM_s$ is a square-integrable martingale with $E(\int_0^TH\,dM)^2=E\int_0^TH_s^2\,dA_s$; extend only to predictable $H$ with $E\int H^2dA<\infty$ by $L^2$ completion. For finitely many subject processes with no simultaneous jumps, prove zero cross optional/predictable bracket under the common filtration and continuous compensators. **B:** one-event interval, delayed entry, tied jumps. **Requires:** existing continuous-time filtration/adaptation/predictability definitions and conditional expectation; AC inherited where used. **Sources:** Stanford Units 8–10 pp.1–5,12–14; some proofs referred elsewhere; Mai Zhou pp.1–7 supplemental only. Local proof; discrete compensator/Brownian Itô cannot substitute.

### SME-31 — Independent-subject terminal influence limits
**Pages:** independent-subject-terminal-influence-limits / independent-subject-terminal-influence-limits-examples. **A, new earlier Cat-2 supplier:** Let $O_i$ be iid subjects and $\phi(O_i)\in\mathbb R^d$ satisfy $E\phi=0$, finite covariance $\Sigma$, and a separately proved expansion $\sqrt n(\hat\theta-\theta)=n^{-1/2}\sum_{i=1}^n\phi(O_i)+o_P(1)$. Then the published multivariate iid CLT and Slutsky give $N_d(0,\Sigma)$. For $G$ independent iid groups with $n_g/n\to\rho_g\in(0,1)$, if the expansion is $\sum_g\sqrt{n/n_g}\,n_g^{-1/2}\sum_{i=1}^{n_g}\phi_g(O_{gi})+o_P(1)$, each $E\phi_g=0$, and covariances are finite, the limit covariance is $\sum_g\rho_g^{-1}\operatorname{Var}(\phi_g)$. This theorem never supplies an influence expansion: each consumer must prove its own risk-set/random-denominator remainder. Terminal finite-dimensional only; no continuous-time or functional martingale CLT. **B:** iid influence sum, group variance, uncontrolled denominator. **Requires:** SME-30 only where martingale integrals are used; published iid multivariate CLT/Slutsky; MSF-12 only for stochastic-order/vector Slutsky algebra (AC inherited from CLT). Current authored A-page supplier interfaces also require `pathwise-differentiability-influence-functions-and-efficient-scores`, `robust-covariance-and-high-dimensional-stability`. **Sources:** Stanford Units 11/13 state process CLTs but refer proofs to Fleming–Harrington; not imported.

### SME-32 — Nelson–Aalen, Kaplan–Meier, Greenwood
**Pages:** nelson-aalen-kaplan-meier-greenwood / nelson-aalen-kaplan-meier-greenwood-examples. **A:** For right-censored iid subjects let $N_i(t)=1\{U_i\le t,\delta_i=1\}$, $Y_i(t)=1\{U_i\ge t\}$, $N=\sum_iN_i$, $Y=\sum_iY_i$, $dN(t)=\sum_i dN_i(t)$, and fix left/right endpoint conventions. Define $\hat\Lambda(t)=\int_{[0,t]}1\{Y(u)>0\}Y(u)^{-1}dN(u)$, $\hat S(t)=\prod_{u\le t:Y(u)>0}(1-dN(u)/Y(u))$, and Greenwood estimate $\hat S(t)^2\sum_{u\le t}dN(u)/\{Y(u)(Y(u)-dN(u))\}$ only where denominators are positive. Greenwood is a first-order asymptotic variance estimate, not an exact finite-sample identity. Under independent censoring and finite event-time support, prove consistency by risk-set probabilities and induction at event times, only through horizons with positive event-survival and censoring-survival probabilities. For continuous time, state the pointwise influence expansion $\sqrt n(\hat S(t)-S(t))=n^{-1/2}\sum_i\phi_i(t)+o_P(1)$, $\phi_i(t)=-S(t)\int_0^t s_0(u)^{-1}dM_i(u)$, $s_0(u)=E[Y_i(u)]>0$, and require a proved random-risk-set remainder plus square-integrability before SME-31 applies. No process bands. **B:** hand risk set/support loss. **Requires:** SME-28/30/31; MSF-02 statistic/estimator definition and MSF-14 pointwise delta. **Sources:** Stanford Units 3 pp.1–12 and 12 pp.2–14; Yu pp.30–38 finite-support proof; NIST pp.103–104. Functional proof not read.

### SME-33 — Logrank and weighted logrank
**Pages:** logrank-and-weighted-logrank / logrank-and-weighted-logrank-examples. **A:** For two groups $g\in\{0,1\}$, define pooled risk count $Y_j=Y_{0j}+Y_{1j}$, group-1 event count $O_j$, tied event count $d_j$, and logrank score $U=\sum_j(O_j-d_jY_{1j}/Y_j)$. Under the conditional hypergeometric null define variance $V=\sum_jY_{1j}Y_{0j}d_j(Y_j-d_j)/\{Y_j^2(Y_j-1)\}$, taking the deterministic zero contribution when $Y_j=1$; state separate tie rules if event causes are not exchangeable. Define weighted score by a bounded predictable weight $w(t)$. Exact Fisher randomization validity is a separate sharp-null assignment theorem. For a terminal equal-hazard normal limit, require independent groups, conditionally independent censoring, $n_1/n\to\rho\in(0,1)$ and $n_0/n\to1-\rho$, positive risk support through fixed $\tau$, bounded predictable weights and a Lindeberg/no-large-jump condition. Define $\bar z(t)$ as the limit of the risk-set treatment fraction; prove $U_n/\sqrt n=n^{-1/2}\sum_i\int_0^\tau w(t)(Z_i-\bar z(t))\,dM_i(t)+o_P(1)$ by uniform risk-set convergence and SME-30 isometry, then use SME-31. No process CLT. **B:** crossing hazards and design randomization. **Requires:** SME-12/28/30/31; MSF-02 randomized-test definition, MSF-08 valid p-value; MSF-20 GC only after verifying its measurability/envelope/entropy hypotheses. **Sources:** Stanford Unit 4 pp.1–13, Unit 13 pp.1–12, links Units 5 pp.15–17 and 7 pp.1–5. Local terminal expansion required.

### SME-34 — Cox PH and partial likelihood
**Pages:** cox-ph-and-partial-likelihood / cox-ph-and-partial-likelihood-examples. **A:** Define a Cox model by conditional event intensity $Y_i(t)\lambda_0(t)e^{\beta_0^TZ_i(t)}$ on $[0,\tau]$, with predictable covariates, absolutely continuous baseline cumulative hazard $\Lambda_0(t)=\int_0^t\lambda_0(u)\,du$ with nonnegative integrable hazard density $\lambda_0$, conditional independent censoring and no tied failures for the displayed partial likelihood. Define hazard ratio $e^{\beta^T(z-z')}$, not a risk ratio. Let $R(t)=\{j:U_j\ge t\}$ and give the no-tie partial likelihood $\prod_{i:\delta_i=1}e^{\beta^TZ_i(U_i)}/\sum_{j\in R(U_i)}e^{\beta^TZ_j(U_i)}$; state exact tied-event likelihood separately from Breslow/Efron approximations. For iid independent subjects, fixed dimension, bounded covariates, positive risk denominators on $[0,\tau]$, nonsingular limiting information, correct PH model and a consistent score root, set $s^{(r)}(t)=E[Y_i(t)Z_i(t)^{\otimes r}e^{\beta_0^TZ_i(t)}]$, $\bar z=s^{(1)}/s^{(0)}$, and $I=\int_0^\tau\{s^{(2)}-s^{(1)}s^{(1)T}/s^{(0)}\}\lambda_0(t)\,dt\succ0$. Prove $\sqrt n(\hat\beta-\beta_0)=I^{-1}n^{-1/2}\sum_i\int_0^\tau(Z_i-\bar z(t))\,dM_i(t)+o_P(1)$. Establish fixed-time risk-set convergence uniform in the compact finite-dimensional parameter set and expected squared-error risk-set convergence integrated against $\lambda_0(t)dt$, Hessian convergence to $-I$ uniform in parameter, square-integrability and negligible random-$\bar Z_n$ replacement before invoking SME-31. Arbitrary bounded predictable paths need no time-class restriction for these integrated laws or the stated inference expansion; uniform-in-time risk-sum convergence is a separate stronger conclusion only under verified pointwise-measurability and finite-bracketing hypotheses. No generic MSF-15 Cox result. **B:** risk-set likelihood/time-varying hazard ratio. **Requires:** SME-28/30/31; MSF-01/02; MSF-20 GC only if its exact hypotheses are verified. **Sources:** Stanford Units 5 pp.1–14,23; 7 pp.1–16; 15 pp.1–12; 16 pp.1–8; Yu pp.58–63 discrete-time caveat. Local proof required; score summands are not iid before risk-set control.

### SME-35 — Competing risks and cumulative incidence
**Pages:** competing-risks-and-cumulative-incidence / competing-risks-and-cumulative-incidence-examples. **A:** Define observed failure time $T=\min_kT_k$, cause $D=\arg\min_kT_k$ (state no simultaneous causes or an explicit tie rule), all-cause survival $S(t)=P(T>t)$, cause-specific hazard measure by $d\Lambda_k(u)=P(T\in du,D=k\mid T\ge u)$, and cumulative incidence $F_k(t)=P(T\le t,D=k)$. Prove $F_k(t)=\int_{[0,t]}S(u-)\,d\Lambda_k(u)$; in the absolutely continuous case $d\Lambda_k(u)=\lambda_k(u)du$, this is $\int_0^tS(u)\lambda_k(u)\,du$. Define Aalen–Johansen by $\hat S(t)=\prod_{u\le t}(1-\sum_kdN_k(u)/Y(u))$ and $\hat F_k(t)=\sum_{u\le t}\hat S(u-)dN_k(u)/Y(u)$, with risk-set support stated. This is an estimator definition, not a limit theorem here. Latent joint failure times are not identified from the observed first cause without added assumptions; treating other causes as censored does not make $1-\hat S_k$ the observed cause-$k$ CIF. **B:** competing mortality alters cause CIF. **Requires:** SME-28/30/32; MSF-01/02; counting-process and product-limit clauses. **Sources:** Stanford competing-risk slides pp.2–12 only; no second full source, so no variance/process limit.

### SME-36 — Calibration and measurement uncertainty
**Pages:** measurement-calibration-and-uncertainty / measurement-calibration-and-uncertainty-examples. **A:** Define measurand $q=f(x_1,\ldots,x_d)$, measured input estimates $\hat x$, corrections/reference standards, and input covariance matrix $\Sigma$. Type A denotes evaluation by statistical analysis of repeated observations; Type B denotes evaluation by other information, not random-versus-systematic error categories. For differentiable $f$ near the input estimate, define first-order combined standard uncertainty by $u_c^2=\nabla f(\hat x)^T\Sigma\nabla f(\hat x)$, explicitly an approximation obtained by Taylor linearization. Include covariance and calibration/reference uncertainty; a later reading of a calibrated instrument may add new repeatability uncertainty. This quantity is not automatically a confidence interval or coverage statement. **B:** calibration curve with uncertain reference standards and batch drift. **Requires:** SME-01; published covariance/Taylor clauses; MSF-14 only if an asymptotic statistical delta limit is claimed. **Sources:** GUM main clauses 1–8 PDF pp.13–39/printed 1–27, full 2026 amendment pp.1–5; NIST process pp.49–85,208–238,243–283. Monte Carlo excluded.

### SME-37 — Detection and quantification limits
**Pages:** detection-and-quantification-limits / detection-and-quantification-limits-examples. **A:** Define $L_C$ as a decision threshold whose blank false-positive probability is $\alpha$, $L_D$ as the concentration giving false-negative probability $\beta$ under the specified signal/blank law, and $L_Q$ as the concentration meeting a declared precision criterion. In the NIST known-blank model with blank $N(0,\sigma_0^2)$, signal $N(c,\sigma_0^2)$, $\alpha=\beta=.05$, and $CV\le k_Q=.10$, derive $L_C=z_{.95}\sigma_0\approx1.645\sigma_0$, $L_D=(z_{.95}+z_{.95})\sigma_0\approx3.29\sigma_0$, and $L_Q=\sigma_0/k_Q=10\sigma_0$, conditional on the equal-known-variance normal model. Estimated blank, heteroscedasticity or a different precision criterion changes the calculation. Below-limit observations are censored measurements and require a measurement likelihood/model. **B:** known/estimated blank, heteroscedasticity. **Requires:** published normal/CDF definitions; MSF-08/10 only for an explicitly calibrated decision/interval. Current authored A-page supplier interfaces also require `brownian-motion-construction-and-continuity`. **Sources:** NIST SP958 PDF pp.177–179/printed 164–166. Official Currie PDF HTTP 403; not read.

### SME-38 — Method agreement
**Pages:** method-agreement-and-limits / method-agreement-and-limits-examples. **A:** For paired measurements $A,B$ on the same target under a declared population of measurement conditions, define difference $D=A-B$, bias $\mu_D=E[D]$, and the conventional rounded normal-reference population limits of agreement $\mu_D\pm1.96\sigma_D$. If $D\sim N(\mu_D,\sigma_D^2)$ with $\sigma_D>0$, the exact central 95% quantiles for individual differences are $\mu_D\pm z_{.975}\sigma_D$, where $z_{.975}=\Phi^{-1}(.975)$; $1.96$ is the customary rounded approximation. If $\sigma_D=0$, both quantiles equal $\mu_D$ and their singleton interval has probability one. These limits are not a 95% confidence interval for either endpoint. Sample plug-in limits estimate those population quantiles; no exact endpoint coverage is claimed. Repeated measures require a covariance/random-effects model and are not iid paired differences. Agreement is distinct from correlation and calibration. **B:** correlated offset methods and repeated measures. **Requires:** SME-01; published normal and covariance clauses (AC inherited from normal law). **Sources:** full Bland–Altman 1986 reproduction pp.1–9. One complete source only; no endpoint coverage.

### SME-39 — Batch and measurement-process variation
**Pages:** measurement-batch-and-process-variation / measurement-batch-and-process-variation-examples. **A:** Define batch/day/operator indices, repeatability as within-condition variation, reproducibility as variation across stated conditions, and stability as constancy of the measurement process over time. In the declared nested model $Y_{ijk}=\mu+\tau_i+B_{ij}+\epsilon_{ijk}$, with independent mean-zero random batch effects of variance $\sigma_B^2$ and repeat errors of variance $\sigma_\epsilon^2$, derive $\operatorname{Cov}(Y_{ijk},Y_{ij\ell})=\sigma_B^2$ for $k\ne\ell$ and $\operatorname{Var}(\bar Y_{ij\cdot})=\sigma_B^2+\sigma_\epsilon^2/m_{ij}$. State when batch/operator is fixed versus random from the target and sampling design. Pool calibration curves only under an explicit common-curve/equivalence model; otherwise report batch-specific descriptions. **B:** day-specific slope drift and check standards. **Requires:** SME-07/36; MSF-01 parametric-model and MSF-02 estimator definitions only if inferential claims are made; published covariance algebra. **Sources:** NIST process pp.208–238,243–283; Duke Lectures 1,5–7 only matching exact models.

### SME-40 — Stationary means and sample autocovariance
**Pages:** stationary-time-series-moments-and-autocovariance / stationary-time-series-moments-and-autocovariance-examples. **A:** A process is strictly stationary when all finite-dimensional laws are invariant under time shifts; it is weakly stationary when $E|X_t|^2<\infty$, the mean is constant, and $\gamma(h)=\operatorname{Cov}(X_{t+h},X_t)$ depends only on lag. For weak stationarity prove $\operatorname{Var}(\bar X_n)=n^{-2}\sum_{|h|<n}(n-|h|)\gamma(h)$. If $\sum_{h\in\mathbb Z}|\gamma(h)|<\infty$, derive $\operatorname{Var}(\bar X_n)=O(n^{-1})$ and $L^2$ consistency for the mean; no mixing or CLT follows. Define the biased sample ACVF $\hat\gamma_n(h)=n^{-1}\sum_{t=1}^{n-|h|}(X_t-\bar X_n)(X_{t+|h|}-\bar X_n)$, symmetrically for negative $h$; zero extension makes its Toeplitz quadratic forms nonnegative. The $1/(n-|h|)$ rescaling need not be positive semidefinite. Birkhoff supplies a separate almost-sure/L1 result only under strict stationarity, ergodicity and integrability. **B:** uncorrelated dependent data and indefinite rescaling. **Requires:** SME-01; published covariance identities; Birkhoff only when its exact hypotheses are met. **Sources:** ETH notes full pp.1–57, esp.2–9,13–14; Wang §§2.1–5.7 pp.14–89, Thms.2.2–2.3/Lemma 2.3 p.47. Wang CLT is not used.

### SME-41 — Spectral measures and periodogram bias
**Pages:** spectral-measures-and-periodogram-bias / spectral-measures-and-periodogram-bias-examples. **A:** For weakly stationary $X_t$ on $\mathbb Z$, positive-definiteness of $\gamma$ and the LCA Bochner theorem give a unique finite positive spectral measure $F$ on the circle with $\gamma(h)=\int e^{ih\lambda}F(d\lambda)$. The theorem's continuity/AC+DC hypotheses must be carried; a spectral density exists only if $F\ll d\lambda$, and $\sum_h|\gamma(h)|<\infty$ gives $f(\lambda)=(2\pi)^{-1}\sum_h\gamma(h)e^{-ih\lambda}$ under the declared frequency convention. Define, for known mean, $I_n(\lambda)=(2\pi n)^{-1}|\sum_{t=1}^n(X_t-\mu)e^{-it\lambda}|^2$. Expanding the finite square proves $E I_n$ is Fejér-kernel convolution of $F$ (or $f$ when it exists). For a nonnegative normalized smoothing kernel on the circle, bandwidth $b_n\to0$, $nb_n\to\infty$, and $f$ continuous at the target frequency, prove the approximate-identity bias limit. No raw-periodogram CLT or interval coverage. **B:** atomic sinusoid versus AR(1). **Requires:** SME-40; existing thm-bochner-theorem-for-lca-groups and Fourier convention (AC+DC). **Sources:** Wang Thm.4.2/Cor.4.2 p.63; ETH pp.36–44; Spodarev 2024 pp.16–25/Lemma 2.10.

### SME-42 — ARMA roots, spectra and inference
**Pages:** arma-causality-invertibility-and-spectra / arma-causality-invertibility-and-spectra-examples. **A:** Define ARMA(p,q) by $\phi(B)(X_t-\mu)=\theta(B)\epsilon_t$, $\phi(z)=1-\sum_{j=1}^p\phi_jz^j$, $\theta(z)=1+\sum_{j=1}^q\theta_jz^j$; white noise means centered, finite positive variance and zero covariance at nonzero lags, not iid. Require normalized coprime $\phi,\theta$ after common-factor cancellation. Causal representation with absolutely summable coefficients exists iff $\phi$ has no zero in $|z|\le1$; invertible representation iff $\theta$ has no zero there. A stationary ARMA solution with $\phi\ne0$ on the unit circle has spectral density $f(\lambda)=\sigma^2(2\pi)^{-1}|\theta(e^{-i\lambda})|^2/|\phi(e^{-i\lambda})|^2$, whether or not the displayed representation is causal/invertible. **B:** common factor, unit root, dependent white noise. **Requires:** SME-40/41; existing Cramer–Wold/CLT only for the separate iid-innovation inference clauses (AC). Current authored A-page supplier interfaces also require `gaussian-orthogonal-decomposition-and-quadratic-forms`. **Sources:** Wang Thms.3.1–3.2 pp.52–55, Thm.4.4 pp.65–66; ETH pp.14–34. Correct printed converse typo; exclude Thm.2.6's incorrect root claim.


**Additional planned inference contracts, proved locally from existing suppliers.** These strengthen the methods for specific iid-innovation models; they are not generic consequences of weak stationarity or white noise. Assume AC as inherited from the probability suppliers.

- `thm-stable-linear-process-sample-mean-clt`: for $X_t-\mu=\sum_{j\ge0}\psi_j\varepsilon_{t-j}$, two-sided iid centered innovations with $0<\sigma^2=E\varepsilon_0^2<\infty$, and $\sum_j\sqrt{j+1}|\psi_j|<\infty$, define $\Psi=\sum_j\psi_j$. Then $\sqrt n(\bar X_n-\mu)\Rightarrow N(0,\sigma^2\Psi^2)$. Causal stable ARMA coefficients decay exponentially and meet the summability premise. Prove existence of the series by Tonelli/L2, and write $\sum_{t=1}^n(X_t-\mu)=\Psi\sum_{t=1}^n\varepsilon_t+R_n$. Minkowski gives $\|R_n\|_2\le\sigma\sum_j|\psi_j|\sqrt{2j}$, hence $R_n/\sqrt n\to0$ in L2; use `thm-lindeberg-levy-iid-central-limit-theorem` and MSF-12. If $\sigma^2\Psi^2>0$ and a variance estimator is proved consistent, Slutsky yields the standardized normal limit and asymptotic interval coverage. Do not imply a variance estimator is consistent merely by naming it, or standardize the zero-variance case.
- `thm-fixed-order-autoregressive-ols-martingale-limit`: for fixed p, a strictly stationary ergodic AR(p) solution $X_t=\phi^TY_t+\varepsilon_t$, $Y_t=(X_{t-1},\ldots,X_{t-p})$, with iid centered innovations independent of the past, $0<\sigma^2<\infty$, and $\Gamma=E[Y_tY_t^T]$ finite positive definite, OLS has $\sqrt n(\hat\phi_n-\phi)\Rightarrow N_p(0,\sigma^2\Gamma^{-1})$. Define a measurable fallback on singular sample Gram matrices and prove its probability tends to zero. Ergodicity gives sample Gram convergence. For each nonzero a, the normalized array $a^TY_t\varepsilon_t/\sqrt{n\sigma^2a^T\Gamma a}$, padded by zeros after n, has predictable variance clock tending to one. Its **unconditional** Lindeberg sum tends to zero by dominated convergence and finite $E[(a^TY_t\varepsilon_t)^2]$, exactly matching the existing `thm-martingale-central-limit-theorem`. Apply `thm-cramer-wold-device`, then MSF-12 and continuity of matrix inversion. The residual mean-square estimator of $\sigma^2$ is consistent by expanding the residual square, the innovation SLLN and Gram convergence; this supplies plug-in fixed-order Wald calibration. Do not condition on the entire lag-design matrix as if it were a fixed exogenous design, or infer validity after data-selected order. The same argument applies with a predictable constant intercept added to Y and with the corresponding parameter dimension and positive-definite Gram matrix. For stable AR(p) with unknown mean, this supplies a fitted innovation variance and slope vector; prove that $\hat\tau^2=\hat\sigma^2/(1-\sum_j\hat\phi_j)^2$ is consistent by continuity and the nonzero stable-model denominator. This gives an actual plug-in long-run variance for the preceding mean interval, without assuming a generic HAC estimator or later computational statistics.

The existing `thm-stationary-process-birkhoff-ergodic-limit`, `thm-kolmogorov-iid-l1-strong-law`, scalar iid/martingale CLTs and `thm-cramer-wold-device` are actual local prerequisite bodies for these routes. Their hypotheses and AC convention were checked. These are planned local proof obligations, not completed item proofs or claims that the source notes supplied these exact arguments. No generic ACVF/periodogram or mixing-process CLT is introduced.

### SME-43 — Finite-history linear prediction
**Pages:** linear-prediction-for-dependent-data / linear-prediction-for-dependent-data-examples. **A:** For weakly stationary $X_t\in L^2$, define the finite-history predictor of $X_{n+h}$ as the $L^2$ projection onto $\operatorname{span}\{1,X_1,\ldots,X_n\}$. The existing affine L2 theorem supplies a unique predictor class even if coefficients are nonunique. If centered history covariance $\Gamma$ is nonsingular, derive $\Gamma a=\gamma_h$, intercept $\mu(1-\sum_i a_i)$, and projection error variance $\gamma(0)-a^T\gamma_h$ for the corresponding stationary lag vector. If $\Gamma$ is singular, state only the unique predictor class/minimizer set from the supplier. Joint Gaussianity makes the projection equal $E[X_{n+h}\mid X_1,\ldots,X_n]$; otherwise conditional expectation may be nonlinear. Optional Durbin–Levinson is a procedure with covariance input, initialization and updates; separately prove by induction that it solves successive Toeplitz systems and returns projection-error variances, requiring positive-definite leading blocks. **B:** singular covariance/Gaussian contrast. **Requires:** SME-40; existing thm-normal-equations-for-best-affine-l2-prediction. **Sources:** Wang §5 pp.67–75; Dewei notes; existing supplier resolves incomplete uniqueness exercise.

### SME-44 — State-space model laws
**Pages:** state-space-models-as-probability-laws / state-space-models-as-probability-laws-examples. **A:** For finite horizon $T$, define standard-Borel state spaces $E_0,\ldots,E_T$, observation spaces $F_1,\ldots,F_T$, initial law $\mu_0$, jointly measurable transition probability kernels $K_t(x_{t-1},dx_t)$, and jointly measurable observation kernels $G_t(x_t,dy_t)$. The joint law is exactly $\mu_0(dx_0)\prod_{t=1}^TK_t(x_{t-1},dx_t)G_t(x_t,dy_t)$; this is the general model contract consumed by BCS-38. Separately define the linear-Gaussian subclass $x_0\sim N(m_0,P_0)$, $x_t=F_tx_{t-1}+q_t$, $y_t=H_tx_t+r_t$, where $q_t\sim N(0,Q_t)$, $r_t\sim N(0,R_t)$, all noises are centered and mutually independent across times and from $x_0$, and $P_0,Q_t,R_t$ are positive semidefinite. This exact clause is consumed by BCS-39; an observed innovation covariance must be positive definite where inverted. State evolution alone is not a joint model. No filtering algorithm, posterior recursion or convergence claim. **B:** same state dynamics with distinct observation kernels; singular state covariance and positive innovation covariance. **Interfaces:** def-general-state-space-model-by-kernels; def-linear-gaussian-state-space-model-with-independent-noises. **Requires:** SME-01/40; MSF-01 parametric-model only if indexed; existing `def-standard-borel-space`, `lem-finite-products-of-standard-borel-spaces-are-standard-borel`, `def-measure-kernel-and-probability-kernel`, and `thm-ionescu-tulcea-construction-of-a-markov-chain` (AC). For the independent linear-Gaussian subclass, reuse `def-independent-random-elements`, `thm-independent-random-elements-have-product-joint-law`, `def-multivariate-normal-law`, `lem-characteristic-function-of-a-multivariate-normal-law`, `lem-characteristic-functions-under-affine-maps-and-independent-sums`, `thm-uniqueness-of-a-law-from-its-characteristic-function`, and `thm-cramer-wold-device` (AC for Ionescu–Tulcea and the Gaussian-law/uniqueness/Cramér–Wold suppliers). **Sources:** ETH pp.46–48; Wang §9.1 pp.146–150, focused units read.

### SME-45 — Random fields and variograms
**Pages:** random-fields-covariance-and-variograms / random-fields-covariance-and-variograms-examples. **A:** Define a real random field as $(X(s))_{s\in T}$ on $T\subseteq\mathbb R^d$; separately state coordinate measurability and joint measurability when spatial integration is used. Define strict stationarity by shift-invariant finite-dimensional laws, wide stationarity by constant mean and covariance $C(s,t)=C(t-s)$, isotropy by dependence only on $\|t-s\|$, and order-two intrinsic stationarity by stationary increments with finite second moments. A covariance kernel is positive semidefinite by the variance of every finite linear combination. Given a symmetric positive-semidefinite kernel and mean, consistent Gaussian finite-dimensional laws plus Kolmogorov extension give a field on the cylinder sigma-algebra, not continuous sample paths; both Gaussian and extension suppliers carry AC. For an order-two intrinsically stationary field, define the displacement-only variogram $\gamma(h)=\tfrac12E[(X(s+h)-X(s))^2]$, which is independent of $s$; under wide stationarity $\gamma(h)=C(0)-C(h)$. Prove conditional negative definiteness: for real $a_i$ with $\sum_i a_i=0$, $\sum_{i,j}a_ia_j\gamma(s_i-s_j)\le0$, by expanding the variance of the corresponding finite combination of increments. Bochner representation requires continuous covariance and inherits AC+DC; omit Schoenberg's converse. **B:** invalid $C(h)=1+h^2$, valid $\gamma(h)=h^2$. **Requires:** SME-40/41; published multivariate-normal, FDD, Kolmogorov and LCA Bochner items (AC for Gaussian/Kolmogorov; AC+DC for Bochner). Current authored A-page supplier interfaces also require `brownian-motion-construction-and-continuity`, `mackeys-imprimitivity-theorem`. **Sources:** Spodarev 2024 §§1.1–1.2 pp.3–8; 2009 §§1.3–1.4 pp.14–20, Thm.2.1.1 p.35, Prop.2.2.1 pp.43–46. Use real coefficients to fix printed complex-weight issue.

### SME-46 — Spatial summary estimators
**Pages:** spatial-field-summary-estimators / spatial-field-summary-estimators-examples. **A:** Define bounded windows $W_n\subset\mathbb R^d$ with $|W_n|\to\infty$ and Van Hove boundary ratio $|(W_n+K)\setminus W_n|/|W_n|\to0$ for every compact $K$; require joint measurability and integrability for $\bar X_{W_n}=|W_n|^{-1}\int_{W_n}X(s)\,ds$. Under stationary finite-variance $X$ with absolutely integrable covariance, prove $E\bar X_{W_n}=EX(0)$ and $L^2$ consistency from the covariance double integral/boundary limit. For lattice observations, define lagged sample covariance/variogram estimators and prove only the source-backed asymptotic-unbiasedness result under consistency of the mean estimate; the covariance estimate need not be PSD. Define the regular-grid periodogram convention, derive its expected Fejer convolution, and prove smoothing bias only under the stated continuity/kernel/bandwidth conditions. For fixed spacing $\delta>0$, the general target is the sampled lattice spectrum: prove the density alias identity $g_\delta(\theta)=\delta^{-d}\sum_{k\in\mathbb Z^d}f((\theta+2\pi k)/\delta)$. Preserve the general aliased-target bias result for a bounded density continuous at the target. For the physically normalized raw and smoothed estimates $\delta^d I_n(\delta\omega_0)$ and $\delta^d\widetilde f_{n,b_n}(\delta\omega_0)$, recover the unaliased continuous-space value only under the exact zero-noncentral-alias condition, with the periodized density bounded and continuous at the target; a continuous spectrum compactly supported inside the Nyquist cube is a proved sufficient case. Explicitly refute the source's unconditional unaliased fixed-grid target. No variance consistency, weak consistency or CLT. **B:** boundary-dominated window and non-PSD covariance. **Requires:** SME-45; published covariance and Bochner clauses (AC+DC for Bochner); published linear Lebesgue change of variables and Radon regularity for the proved sampling alias bridge. **Sources:** Spodarev 2024 Thm.2.5, Lemmas 2.2/2.8/2.10 pp.16–20; Stanford Lecture 6 is plug-in caution, not full asymptotic proof.

### SME-47 — Kriging
**Pages:** kriging-as-best-linear-prediction / kriging-as-best-linear-prediction-examples. **A:** For observations $Z=(X(s_1),\ldots,X(s_n))^T$, target $X(s_0)$, covariance matrix $\Sigma$, and covariance vector $c_0$, define simple kriging with known mean $m$ by $\hat X_0=m(s_0)+\lambda^T(Z-m)$. If $\Sigma\succ0$, minimization of mean-square prediction error gives $\lambda=\Sigma^{-1}c_0$ and error variance $C(0)-c_0^T\Sigma^{-1}c_0$. For Gaussian fields with known mean/covariance this is the conditional mean; otherwise it is only the best affine predictor. Ordinary kriging treats an unknown constant mean by imposing $1^T\lambda=1$; universal kriging with trend $p(s)^T\beta$ imposes $P^T\lambda=p(s_0)$, where $P$ is the finite observation-site design matrix. Give the Lagrange/KKT systems and require nonsingular $\Sigma$, full column rank $P$, and the corresponding augmented-system invertibility; for variogram formulations state conditional negative definiteness separately. Plug-in covariance/trend estimates do not retain oracle optimality. **B:** singular covariance, rank failure and ordinary/simple variance difference. **Requires:** SME-43/45; published least-squares/affine projection; MSF-03 only for explicit prediction-risk terminology (AC for Gaussian law). **Sources:** Spodarev 2024 §§3.1–3.2 pp.26–48; Sun Stanford Lecture 5 full. Exclude Lemma 3.28's finite-site rank error.

### SME-48 — Poisson and Cox point processes
**Pages:** poisson-and-cox-point-process-models / poisson-and-cox-point-process-models-examples. **A:** On a stated locally compact second-countable Borel space $S$, define a point process as a random locally finite counting measure $\eta$; distinguish simple from multiplicity-bearing processes. Define intensity measure $\nu(B)=E\eta(B)$. State the Poisson-process existence theorem for locally finite $\sigma$-finite $\nu$, with proof by independent Poisson counts on a countable precompact partition and consistency/extension. A Poisson process has independent counts on disjoint measurable sets with $N(B)\sim\operatorname{Poisson}(\nu(B))$; a Cox process is conditionally Poisson given a random directing measure $\xi$, with the conditional law a measurable probability kernel. State Campbell $E\eta(f)=\int f\,d\nu$, Poisson Laplace functional $E e^{-\eta(f)}=\exp[-\int(1-e^{-f})d\nu]$, and Mecke identity $\displaystyle E\sum_{x\in\eta}h(x,\eta-\delta_x)=\int E[h(x,\eta)]\,\nu(dx)$ for measurable $h\ge0$, including the source converse characterization under its sigma-finite/local-finiteness hypotheses. For a Cox process and $\varphi\ge0$, $\eta(\varphi)=\int\varphi\,d\eta$, assume $\varphi\in L^1(\nu)\cap L^2(\nu)$ and $E[\xi(\varphi)^2]<\infty$; conditioning gives $E[\eta(\varphi)^2]=E[\xi(\varphi^2)+\xi(\varphi)^2]$ and $\operatorname{Var}\eta(\varphi)=\nu(\varphi^2)+\operatorname{Var}(\xi(\varphi))$. State the exact equivalence of finite second moments only with these hypotheses. Campbell/Laplace/Mecke proofs use simple functions, monotone convergence and the Poisson count law. Exclude directing-law uniqueness (source's final analytic uniqueness step is externally cited) and diffuse-intensity simplicity unless separately proved. **B:** random/deterministic intensity with the same mean and different count variance. **Requires:** SME-30 only where compensator is used; published measure/kernel/product integration items; no point-process law is supplied by ordinary counting measure. **Sources:** Last–Penrose Chs.1–4 pp.13–49, Ch.13 pp.139–147; Baddeley Lecture 1 pp.1–25. Unread invoked Ch.6 excluded.

### SME-49 — Spatial summaries and Palm laws
**Pages:** spatial-point-process-summaries-and-palm-laws / spatial-point-process-summaries-and-palm-laws-examples. **A:** For a stationary locally finite point process $\eta$ on $\mathbb R^d$ with finite positive intensity $\rho$, use the full Palm law $P^0$ and the recentering convention $(\theta_x\mu)(B)=\mu(B+x)$. Define $P^0$ by the refined Campbell identity $E\int h(x,\theta_x\eta)\,\eta(dx)=\rho\int_{\mathbb R^d}\int h(x,\mu)\,P^0(d\mu)\,dx$ for every nonnegative jointly measurable $h$, not by conditioning on a zero-probability event; this law retains the distinguished atom at the origin. If $\eta=\sum_i\delta_{X_i}$, define $\alpha^{(2)}(A\times B)=E\sum_{i\ne j}1_A(X_i)1_B(X_j)$, with distinct atom indices counting multiplicities; distinct locations suffice only for a simple process. On the locally square-integrable second-order route, define the reduced second factorial moment measure by $\alpha^{(2)}_{\rm red}(D)=E\sum_{i\ne j}1_Q(X_i)1_D(X_j-X_i)$ for the unit-volume cube $Q=[0,1)^d$, prove $\alpha^{(2)}_{\rm red}(D)=\rho\int(\mu-\delta_0)(D)\,P^0(d\mu)$, and define pair-correlation density $g$ only if $\alpha^{(2)}_{\rm red}(du)=\rho^2g(u)\,du$. For $r\ge0$, with the closed Euclidean ball convention, define Ripley's $K(r)=\rho^{-1}\int(\mu-\delta_0)(B(0,r))\,P^0(d\mu)=\rho^{-2}\alpha^{(2)}_{\rm red}(B(0,r))$; this removes one distinguished occurrence, retaining any other co-located atoms. For simple processes it equals $\rho^{-1}E^0[\mu(B(0,r)\setminus\{0\})]$. If $g$ exists, prove $K(r)=\int_{B(0,r)}g(u)\,du$; for homogeneous Poisson prove $K(r)=|B(0,r)|$ via the Mecke–Slivnyak full-Palm law $P^0=P(\eta+\delta_0\in\cdot)$. Define edge-corrected K estimators as procedures with the correction weight stated, with no automatic unbiasedness claim. For compact convex $W$ with nonempty interior and dilations $W_n=a_nW$, state the Last–Penrose result $\eta(W_n)/|W_n|\to E[\eta(Q)\mid\mathcal I]$ in $L^1$, where $Q$ is a unit-volume reference set and $\mathcal I$ is the translation-invariant sigma-algebra; ergodicity makes the limit $\rho$. No rate/CLT. **B:** edge effects, absent pair-correlation density, and a uniformly translated lattice showing why Palm configurations must be recentered; compare deletion of one occurrence with removal of all origin atoms when multiplicities are present. **Requires:** SME-48; published measure and ergodic suppliers under their exact conditions. **Sources:** Last–Penrose Ch.8 PDF pp.81–93 (printed pp.69–81), Def.8.6/Prop.8.7 and Thm.8.14; Ch.9 PDF pp.94–103 (printed pp.82–91), Thms.9.1/9.4, formula (9.6) and Exercise 9.9; Baddeley Lectures 2–3 pp.26–56.

### SME-50 — Finite-window spatial likelihood
**Pages:** finite-window-spatial-point-process-likelihoods / finite-window-spatial-point-process-likelihoods-examples. **A:** State bounded observation window $W\subset\mathbb R^d$, reference measure and boundary convention. For a Poisson process with measurable intensity $\lambda_\theta\ge0$ and $\int_W\lambda_\theta(x)dx<\infty$, derive its density relative to unit-rate Poisson measure $P_0$: $dP_\theta/dP_0(\eta)=\exp\{|W|-\int_W\lambda_\theta\}\prod_{x\in\eta\cap W}\lambda_\theta(x)$; hence log likelihood up to a parameter-free constant is $\sum_{x\in\eta\cap W}\log\lambda_\theta(x)-\int_W\lambda_\theta(x)dx$. For homogeneous $\lambda_\rho=\rho\ge0$, if $N(W)>0$, maximize at $\hat\rho=N(W)/|W|$; if $N(W)=0$, the supremum is at $\rho=0$, attained only when the parameter space includes zero. Define finite-window Gibbs density relative to a specified Poisson reference by $Z_\theta^{-1}e^{-H_\theta(\eta)}1_{\{\eta\in\mathcal S_\theta\}}$, requiring support $\mathcal S_\theta$ and $0<Z_\theta<\infty$. Where a Papangelou conditional intensity $\lambda_\theta(u;\eta)$ is defined, define pseudolikelihood as $\prod_{x\in\eta\cap W}\lambda_\theta(x;\eta\setminus\{x\})\exp\{-\int_W\lambda_\theta(u;\eta)\,du\}$, with boundary/exposure convention stated; it is not the joint likelihood and no consistency/asymptotic guarantee is claimed. **B:** Poisson/Strauss exact likelihood versus pseudolikelihood. **Requires:** SME-48; MSF-04 dominated-likelihood definition only for the stated reference-density interpretation. **Sources:** Baddeley Lecture 4 pp.57–73.

### SME-51 — Temporal intensity and likelihood
**Pages:** temporal-point-process-intensity-and-likelihood / temporal-point-process-intensity-and-likelihood-examples. **A:** Define simple locally finite ordered event times $0<T_1<T_2<\cdots$, history filtration $\mathcal F_t$, and nonnegative predictable conditional intensity $\lambda^*(t)$ with $\int_0^T\lambda^*(u)du<\infty$ a.s. on each finite window and compensator contract $N(t)-\int_0^t\lambda^*(u)du$ a martingale. Conditional on history at $t$, derive next-event survival $\exp\{-\int_t^u\lambda^*(s\mid\text{no event after }t)ds\}$ and its density where the cumulative hazard is absolutely continuous. For observed event times $t_1<\cdots<t_m\le T$, derive likelihood relative to unit-rate Poisson reference proportional to $\prod_{i=1}^m\lambda^*(t_i)\exp\{-\int_0^T\lambda^*(u)du\}$, with positive event intensity at observed events and the terminal no-event factor included. Finite cumulative hazard to infinity permits a terminal mass at no further event; next-event a.s. requires infinite cumulative hazard and a non-explosion condition. Marked processes require their conditional mark law factor. Intensity specification alone proves neither existence/stationarity of a realization nor Hawkes stability. **B:** Poisson, Hawkes specification with existence kept separate, and terminating process. **Requires:** SME-30/48; MSF-04 only for the explicit dominated reference likelihood; continuous-time filtration/predictability. **Sources:** Rasmussen full 19 pp., Props.2.1,2.2,3.1; unread/failed source records are not evidence.

### SME-52 — Valid p-values, Bonferroni, simultaneous coverage
**Pages:** valid-p-values-bonferroni-and-simultaneous-coverage / valid-p-values-bonferroni-and-simultaneous-coverage-examples. **A:** Define a finite family $H_{0i}$, true-null index set $I_0(\theta)$, valid p-value by $P_\theta(p_i\le u)\le u$ for every $\theta\in H_{0i}$ and $u\in[0,1]$, FWER $P_\theta(V\ge1)$, and FDR $E_\theta[V/(R\vee1)]$, with $V$ false rejections and $R$ total rejections. For any dependence, rejecting at $p_i\le\alpha/m$ gives strong FWER $\le\alpha$ by union bound. If marginal confidence sets $C_i$ have coverage $P_\theta(\theta_i\in C_i)\ge1-\alpha_i$ and $\sum_i\alpha_i\le\alpha$, prove joint coverage at least $1-\alpha$; no independence is needed. **B:** family choice, true-null set, and dependence. **Requires:** MSF-08/10; published probability subadditivity. **Sources:** models-06 M06-P01/P04; direct finite union proof.

### SME-53 — Holm strong FWER
**Pages:** holm-step-down-familywise-error-control / holm-step-down-familywise-error-control-examples. **A:** Algorithm input is $m$ valid p-values and $\alpha\in[0,1]$: sort $p_{(1)}\le\cdots\le p_{(m)}$, reject sequentially while $p_{(j)}\le\alpha/(m-j+1)$, and stop at the first failure. For $m_0$ true nulls, the earliest-ranked true null has cutoff at most $\alpha/m_0$; if any true null is rejected, that one is rejected. The union bound and p-value validity give strong FWER $\le\alpha$ under arbitrary dependence. **B:** hand family versus Bonferroni. **Requires:** MSF-08; SME-52; published probability subadditivity. **Sources:** Goeman–Solari Thm.1/proof p.5; Holm specialization p.9; dossier complete.

### SME-54 — BH independent FDR
**Pages:** benjamini-hochberg-independent-fdr-control / benjamini-hochberg-independent-fdr-control-examples. **A:** For $q\in(0,1)$, $m$ mutually independent p-values, exactly uniform continuous true-null p-values and arbitrary false-null laws, define $R=\max\{k:p_{(k)}\le kq/m\}$, with $R=0$ if the set is empty, and reject ranks $1,\ldots,R$. Prove $\mathrm{FDR}=E[V/(R\vee1)]\le(m_0/m)q$ by the source leave-one-out/induction lemma conditioning on false-null values. This is FDR control, not FWER control. Discrete superuniform true-null extensions require an additional proof and are not included. **B:** small independent family/FDR-FWER. **Requires:** SME-52; MSF-08; published mutual-independence/product-law supplier. **Sources:** BH95 Thm.1 p.6/Appendix A pp.12–13; BY01 pp.16–17.

### SME-55 — BY arbitrary dependence and BH PRDS
**Pages:** fdr-control-under-dependence / fdr-control-under-dependence-examples. **A:** Define $H_m=\sum_{j=1}^m1/j$. Under arbitrary dependence among valid p-values, the BY step-up rule $p_{(k)}\le kq/(mH_m)$ has FDR $\le(m_0/m)q$, by summing null contributions over rejection counts with weights $1/k$. Separately define PRDS on the true-null coordinates: for each true-null $i$ and every coordinatewise increasing measurable set $D\subseteq[0,1]^m$, $x\mapsto P(P\in D\mid P_i=x)$ has a nondecreasing version on the support. Under this exact condition, ordinary BH $p_{(k)}\le kq/m$ has FDR $\le(m_0/m)q$. Prove by the BY harmonic argument and the source PRDS monotonicity lemma, respectively. Pairwise positive correlation is not PRDS; two-sided Gaussian tests have no blanket guarantee. **B:** available dependence premise and threshold comparison. **Requires:** SME-52/54; MSF-08; exact PRDS definition. Current authored A-page supplier interfaces also require `approximation-algorithms-and-gap-reductions`. **Sources:** BY01 PRDS definition p.4, BY pp.5,18–19, PRDS pp.13–16; full paper.

### SME-56 — Polyhedral selective inference
**Pages:** polyhedral-selective-inference / polyhedral-selective-inference-examples. **A:** Let fixed $Y\sim N_n(\mu,\sigma^2I_n)$, known $\sigma>0$, nonzero fixed $\eta\in\mathbb R^n$, and selection event $S=\{AY\le b\}$ with $P_\mu(S)>0$. Set $T=\eta^TY$, $\theta=\eta^T\mu$, $Z=(I-\eta\eta^T/\|\eta\|^2)Y$; then $T\perp Z$, and conditional on $Z$, the polyhedron restricts $T$ to an interval $[L(Z),U(Z)]$, possibly with infinite endpoints. Conditional on $Z,S$, $T$ is $N(\theta,\sigma^2\|\eta\|^2)$ truncated to that interval. Its conditional CDF pivot $\{\Phi((T-\theta)/s)-\Phi((L-\theta)/s)\}/\{\Phi((U-\theta)/s)-\Phi((L-\theta)/s)\}$, $s=\sigma\|\eta\|$, is Uniform$(0,1)$ conditional on $Z,S$ wherever the truncation normalizer is positive. Invert this pivot for exact selective tests/confidence sets; no unknown-variance, nonlinear/nonpolyhedral selection or selected-family simultaneous guarantee. **B:** choose larger of two normal means. **Requires:** MSF-08/10; published Gaussian orthogonal projection/normal-law clauses (AC inherited). **Sources:** Lee et al. 2016 Lemma 4.1, Prop.4.2, Lemma 5.1, Thms.5.2/6.1/App.A, full article; Fithian–Sun–Taylor focused pp.1–10,15–16.

### SME-57 — Reproducibility and transparent reporting
**Pages:** reproducibility-replication-and-transparent-reporting / reproducibility-replication-and-transparent-reporting-examples. **A:** Adopt the National Academies convention explicitly: computational reproducibility compares results using the same input data, code/computational methods and analysis conditions; replicability compares studies aimed at the same question using each study's new data; generalizability concerns other contexts/populations. Define transparent reporting as identifying data/code where lawful and ethical, computational environment, curation, methods and analysis decisions, exploratory/confirmatory status, and uncertainty. Record that usage varies across fields and this page adopts this convention. These are definitions/recommendations, not a theorem about correctness or future replication; exact reproduction can repeat an error. **B:** same raw data, alternate documented grouping and an independent study with new data. **Requires:** none beyond model/study terminology. **Sources:** NASEM Ch.3 Conclusion 3-1 pp.45–46, discussion pp.46–52, Box 3-2/Finding 3-1 pp.53–54; full chapter. Ioannidis is context only; no PPV theorem.

### SME-58 — Discrete test supermartingales and Ville
**Pages:** discrete-sequential-test-validity-and-ville / discrete-sequential-test-validity-and-ville-examples. **A:** For every $P$ in a null family, let $M_t\ge0$ be an adapted integrable discrete-time supermartingale with $E_PM_0\le1$. Define $\tau=\inf\{t\ge0:M_t\ge1/\alpha\}$, $\alpha\in(0,1)$. Prove the distinct bounds $P(\tau<\infty)\le\alpha$ and $P(\sup_{t\ge0}M_t\ge1/\alpha)\le\alpha$. Obtain the first-crossing bound by bounded optional sampling at $\tau\wedge N$, nonnegativity, and continuity from below. For the supremum bound, use $\gamma_n=\alpha+(1-\alpha)/(n+1)\downarrow\alpha$: its event is $\bigcap_{n\ge1}\{\exists t:M_t\ge1/\gamma_n\}$, so its probability is at most every $\gamma_n$ and hence at most $\alpha$; no attainment of the supremum is assumed. This controls threshold tests at arbitrary stopping times; it does not make a fixed-time p-value valid after repeated peeking. **B:** Under independent Uniform$(0,1)$ p-values from $K$ fresh global-null looks, repeated unadjusted level-$\alpha$ testing rejects with probability $1-(1-\alpha)^K$. **Requires:** MSF-34; published discrete martingale and bounded optional-sampling clauses (AC). **Sources:** Howard 2020 Def.1/Thm.1/proof pp.9–18,45; Wolpert pp.1–5,13–16. Continuous extension excluded.

### SME-59 — Sequential likelihood-ratio tests
**Pages:** sequential-likelihood-ratio-tests / sequential-likelihood-ratio-tests-examples. **A:** Separate three items: a procedure algorithm, two terminal-error bounds, and an iid termination theorem. For simple $H_0:P_0$ versus $H_1:P_1$ on filtration $(\mathcal F_t)$, assume mutually equivalent restrictions $P_0|_{\mathcal F_t}\sim P_1|_{\mathcal F_t}$ for each finite $t$, with consistent density process $L_t=dP_1|_{\mathcal F_t}/dP_0|_{\mathcal F_t}$. For $0<\alpha,\beta<1$, define $\tau=\inf\{t\ge1:L_t\ge1/\alpha\text{ or }L_t\le\beta\}$, decide $H_1$ at the upper boundary and $H_0$ at the lower; nontermination remains possible absent the separate theorem. Under $P_0$, $L_t$ is a nonnegative mean-one martingale; under $P_1$, $1/L_t$ is one. Ville gives $P_0(\tau<\infty,L_\tau\ge1/\alpha)\le\alpha$ and $P_1(\tau<\infty,L_\tau\le\beta)\le\beta$, without finite stopping. For iid observations with mutually equivalent one-observation laws and $Z=\log(dP_1/dP_0)(X)$, if $E_0|Z|,E_1|Z|<\infty$ and $E_0Z<0<E_1Z$, the iid strong law yields $\log L_t/t\to E_0Z<0$ under $P_0$ and $E_1Z>0$ under $P_1$, so $\tau<\infty$ a.s. under each law. Error control and termination are distinct; no expected-time optimality or exact Wald approximate-boundary claim. **B:** Bernoulli simple hypotheses with product likelihood ratio; compute error crossings and verify stopping under the drift conditions. **Requires:** MSF-04 likelihood-ratio RN derivative/change of measure; MSF-34 test-supermartingale/Ville; published bounded optional sampling and iid L1 strong law. **Sources:** Wolpert Duke notes pp. 1–5, 13–16 discuss SPRT and mark Wald cutoffs approximate; Howard 2020 supports the supermartingale boundary argument; existing finite-horizon ex-stopping-a-likelihood-ratio-martingale is only a cross-check. The two-boundary properties and iid termination proof are local.
### SME-60 — Sub-Gaussian mixture confidence sequences
**Pages:** subgaussian-mixture-confidence-sequences / subgaussian-mixture-confidence-sequences-examples. **A:** For adapted real $X_t$, fixed $\mu$, filtration $\mathcal F_t$, known $\sigma^2>0$, assume for every $\lambda\in\mathbb R$, $E[e^{\lambda(X_t-\mu)}\mid\mathcal F_{t-1}]\le e^{\lambda^2\sigma^2/2}$. Define $S_t=\sum_{i=1}^t(X_i-\mu)$, $\bar X_t=t^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^tX_i$, $V_t=t\sigma^2$, and choose $\rho>0,\alpha\in(0,1)$. Mixing the exponential supermartingales over $N(0,1/\rho)$ gives $M_t=\sqrt{\rho/(V_t+\rho)}e^{S_t^2/[2(V_t+\rho)]}$. By completing the square, $M_t\ge1/\alpha$ iff $|S_t|\ge u_\alpha(V_t)$, $u_\alpha(v)=\sqrt{(v+\rho)\log((v+\rho)/(\alpha^2\rho))}$. Thus define $C_t=[\bar X_t-u_\alpha(t\sigma^2)/t,\bar X_t+u_\alpha(t\sigma^2)/t]$, and Ville gives $P_\mu(\mu\in C_t\ \forall t\ge1)\ge1-\alpha$. The proxy must satisfy the conditional mgf bound; marginal sub-Gaussianity or a variance bound alone is insufficient. **B:** Gaussian increments versus invalid marginal-only sub-Gaussian claim. **Requires:** SME-58; MSF-34 mixture/CS; conditional Tonelli. Current authored B-page supplier interfaces also require `concentration-inequalities-and-tail-bounds`. **Sources:** Howard 2021 focused pp.1–9,27–28; Howard 2020 pp.9–18,45.

### SME-61 — Group-sequential alpha spending
**Pages:** group-sequential-alpha-spending / group-sequential-alpha-spending-examples. **A:** Specify maximum $K$, prescheduled information fractions $0<t_1<\cdots<t_K=1$, nondecreasing spending function $A:[0,1]\to[0,\alpha]$ for $\alpha\in(0,1)$ with $A(0)=0,A(1)=\alpha$, and the one-sided canonical Gaussian null vector $Z\sim N_K(0,\Sigma)$ with zero mean and $\Sigma_{jk}=\sqrt{t_j/t_k}$ for $j\le k$ (and symmetry for $j>k$). Set $t_0=0$ and define the boundary algorithm recursively by $\Pr_0(\max_{j\le k}Z_j\ge b_j)=A(t_k)$; when $A(t_k)=A(t_{k-1})$ set $b_k=+\infty$, and when spending increases choose the finite boundary under the stated joint law. This gives exact cumulative type-I spending at each planned look and total $\alpha$; two-sided designs use separately calibrated two-sided crossing events. Exactness is conditional on this joint law and prespecified looks; data-dependent timing/estimated variance requires a new calibration argument. **B:** named spending functions under canonical law. **Requires:** MSF-08; published `def-multivariate-normal-law`, `def-standard-normal-and-normal-laws`, and `lem-normal-density-has-total-mass-one` (AC for the Gaussian-law supplier). **Sources:** DeMets–Lan 1994 pp.2–3,6–7,9; Grayling–Wason–Mander 2018 pp.2–4; Minas Ch.5 5.3–5.3.1 printed pp.72–74.

### SME-62 — Two-stage conditional-error adaptation
**Pages:** two-stage-conditional-error-adaptation / two-stage-conditional-error-adaptation-examples. **A:** For a fixed null law $P_0$ and interim sigma-algebra $\mathcal F_1$, define the original conditional error $A= P_0(\text{original rejection}\mid\mathcal F_1)$, assuming $E_0A\le\alpha$. If an adapted design has conditional probability of any eventual rejection at most $A$ a.s., then $P_0(\text{adapted rejection})\le\alpha$ by the tower property. A sufficient implementation is a stage-2 p-value satisfying $P_0(p_2\le u\mid\mathcal F_1)\le u$ a.s. for every $u\in[0,1]$, with rejection rule $p_2\le A$. Fresh stage-2 data independent under the conditional null is one sufficient route, but the selected-sample-size conditional law must be stated. This covers single-hypothesis sample-size/continuation adaptation; treatment-allocation learning, endpoint selection and multiarm adaptive selection are excluded. **B:** calibrated sample-size adaptation versus changed combination weights without conditional recalibration. **Requires:** MSF-08; published conditional-expectation tower (AC). **Sources:** Sugiura–Uesaka 2008 pp.6–9; Minas Ch.5 5.4.1–5.4.2 printed pp.74–79, complete chapter.

